\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _5479 }{\fldrslt 8-1. PPG core group configuration variables}}\tqr\tldot\tx9360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _5479 }{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF _5479}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _5619 }{\fldrslt 8-2. PPG user group configuration variables}}\tqr\tldot\tx9360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _5619 }{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF _5619}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _5781 }{\fldrslt 8-3. Kannel headers used by PPG}}\tqr\tldot\tx9360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _5781 }{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF _5781}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _6051 }{\fldrslt D-1. Fakesmsc command line options}}\tqr\tldot\tx9360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _6051 }{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF _6051}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _6131 }{\fldrslt E-1. Test_ppg's command line options}}\tqr\tldot\tx9360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _6131 }{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF _6131}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _6254 }{\fldrslt E-2. Test_ppg's configuration file directives}}\tqr\tldot\tx9360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _6254 }{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF _6254}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\pgnrestart\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 1. Introduction}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 1. Introduction}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _80}{\*\bkmkend _80}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 1. Introduction\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter introduces WAP and SMS in general terms, and explains the role of the gateway in WAP and SMS, outlining their duties and features. It also explains why the Kannel project was started in the first place, and why it is open source.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 With hundreds of millions of mobile phones in use all over the world, the market for services targeted at mobile users is mind-bogglingly immense. Even simple services find plenty of users, as long as they're useful or fun. Being able to get news, send e-mail or just be entertained wherever you are is extremely attractive to many.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The hottest technology for implementing mobile services is WAP, short for Wireless Application Protocol. It lets the phone act as a simple web browser, but optimizes the markup language, scripting language, and the transmission protocols for wireless use. The optimized protocols are translated to plain old HTTP by a \i WAP gateway\i0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel is an open source WAP gateway. It attempts to provide this essential part of the WAP infrastructure freely to everyone so that the market potential for WAP services, both from wireless operators and specialized service providers, will be realized as efficiently as possible.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel also works as an SMS gateway for GSM networks. Almost all GSM phones can send and receive SMS messages, so this is a way to serve many more clients than just those using a new WAP phone.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition, Kannel operates as \i Push Proxy Gateway \i0 , or \i PPG\i0 , making possible for content servers to send data to the phones. This is a new type of WAP service, and have many interesting applications. Usually servers know whether some data is new, not the users.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel's quality has been recognized on March 7, 2001 when it was certified (http://www.kannel.org/oldnews.shtml#wapcert) by WAP Forum (http://www.wapforum.org) as the first WAP 1.1 gateway in the world.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Greater quality recognition are the quantity of companies using Kannel to successful connect to a variety of SMSC protocols in lots of countries.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Open Source (http://www.opensource.org) is a way to formalize the principle of openness by placing the source code of a product under a Open Source compliant software license. The BSD license was chosen over other Open Source licenses by the merit of placing the least amount of limitations on what a third party is able to do with the source code. In practice this means that Kannel is going to be a fully-featured WAP implementation and compatible with the maximum number of bearers with special emphasis on SMSC compatibility. The Kannel project was founded by Wapit Ltd in June, 1999.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _98}{\*\bkmkend _98}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Overview of WAP\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 WAP, short for Wireless Application Protocol, is a collection of various languages and tools and an infrastructure for implementing services for mobile phones. Traditionally such services have worked via normal phone calls or short textual messages (e.g., SMS messages in GSM networks). Neither are very efficient to use, nor very user friendly. WAP makes it possible to implement services similar to the World Wide Web.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Unlike marketers claim, WAP does not bring the existing content of the Internet directly to the phone. There are too many technical and other problems for this to ever work properly. The main problem is that Internet content is mainly in the form of HTML pages, and they are written in such way that they require fast connections, fast processors, large memories, big screens, audio output and often also fairly efficient input mechanisms. That's OK, since they hopefully work better for traditional computers and networks that way. However, portable phones have very slow processors, very little memory, abysmal and intermittent bandwidth, and extremely awkward input mechanisms. Most existing HTML pages do not work on mobiles phones, and never will.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 WAP defines a completely new markup language, the Wireless Markup Language (WML), which is simpler and much more strictly defined than HTML. It also defines a scripting language, WMLScript, which all browsers are required to support. To make things even simpler for the phones, it even defines its own bitmap format (Wireless Bitmap, or WBMP).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 HTTP is also too inefficient for wireless use. However, by using a semantically similar binary and compressed format it is possible to reduce the protocol overhead to a few bytes per request, instead of the usual hundreds of bytes. Thus, WAP defines a new protocol stack to be used. However, to make things simpler also for the people actually implementing the services, WAP introduces a gateway between the phones and the servers providing content to the phones.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 Figure 1-1. Logical position of WAP gateway (and PPG) between a phone and a content server.\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "wap-gateway.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\lang1033 The WAP gateway talks to the phone using the WAP protocol stack, and translates the requests it receives to normal HTTP. Thus content providers can use any HTTP servers and utilize existing know-how about HTTP service implementation and administration.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition to protocol translations, the gateway also compresses the WML pages into a more compact form, to save on-the-air bandwidth and to further reduce the phone's processing requirements. It also compiles WMLScript programs into a byte-code format. Latest WAP specifications defines some additional conversions that Kannel is starting to implement, like multipart/form-data, multipart/mixed or MMS content conversion.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel is not just a WAP gateway. It also works as an SMS gateway. Although WAP is the hot and technically superior technology, SMS phones exist in huge numbers and SMS services are thus quite useful. Therefore, Kannel functions simultaneously as both a WAP and an SMS gateway.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _110}{\*\bkmkend _110}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Overview of WAP Push\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Previous chapter explained pull mode of operation: the phone initiates the transaction. There is, however, situations when the server (called in this context a push initiator) should be the initiator, for instance, when it must send a mail notification or a stock quote. For this purpose WAP Forum defined WAP Push.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Push is an application level service, sitting on the top of existing WAP stack. It defines two protocols, OTA and PAP. OTA is a ligthweigth protocol speaking with WAP stack (to be more specific, with WSP), PAP speaks with the push initiator. It defines three kind of XML documents, one for the push data itself and another for protocol purposes (these are called pap document or push control documents).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The server does not simply send push content to the phone, the user would surely not accept, for instance, interrupting of a voice call. Instead it sends a specific XML document, either Service Indication or Service Loading. These inform the user about the content become available, and it is displayed only when it is not interrupting anything. It contains an URL specifying the service and a text for user describing the content. Then the user can decide does he accept push or not.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The push content is send ed to the phones over SMS, but the content is fetched by the phone over IP bearer, for instance CSD or GPRS. Because Push Proxy Gateway tokenises SI and SL documents, it may fit one SMS message (if not, it is segmented for transfer).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Using two bearers seems to be an unnecessary complication. But quite simply, phones currently operate this way. Push over GPRS can only simplify matters.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _117}{\*\bkmkend _117}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Overview of SMS\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 SMS, short messaging service, is a way to send short (160 character) messages from one GSM phone to another. It can also be used to send regular text as well as advanced content like operator logos, ringing tones, business cards and phone configurations.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \i SMS services\i0  are content services initiated by SMS message to certain (usually short) phone number, which then answers with requested content, if available.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 When SMS services are used, the client (mobile terminal) sends an SMS message to certain number, usually a very short specialized number, which points to specific SMS center responsible for that number (plus possibly many others). This SMS center then sends the message onward to specified receiver in intra- or Internet, using an SMS center specific protocol. For example, a Nokia SMS center uses CIMD protocol.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 As practically every different kind of SMS center uses different protocol, an \i SMS gateway\i0  is used to handle connections with SMS centers and to relay them onward in an unified form. Kannel's biggest feature is to abstract each SMSC protocol to a well-known HTTP protocol, simplifying services deployment.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 Figure 1-2. Logical position of SMS gateway between a phone and a content server.\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "sms-gateway.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\lang1033 An SMS gateway can also be used to relay SMS messages from one GSM network to another, if the networks do not roam messages normally.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel works as an SMS gateway, talking with many different kind of SMS centers, and relaying the messages onward to content providers, as HTTP queries. Content providers then answer to this HTTP query and the answer is sent back to mobile terminal, with appropriate SMS center connection using SMS center specific protocol.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition to serving mobile originated (MO) SMS messages Kannel also works as an SMS push gateway - content providers can request Kannel to send SMS messages to terminals. Kannel then determines the correct SMS center to relay the SMS message and sends the SMS message to that SMS center, again using SMS center specific protocol. This way the content provider does not need to know any SMS center specific protocol, just unified Kannel SMS sending interface.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _131}{\*\bkmkend _131}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Features\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _133}{\*\bkmkend _133}\fs28 WAP\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16\lang1033\f0 \'95\tab \fs20 Fully compliant with WAP 1.1 specification\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Already implements some WAP 1.2 and even WAP 2.0 features.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _141}{\*\bkmkend _141}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 WAP Push\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16\lang1033\f0 \'95\tab \fs20 Supports WAP Push SI and SL\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 ...\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _149}{\*\bkmkend _149}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 SMS\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16\lang1033\f0 \'95\tab \fs20 Supports a variety of SMSC protocols, namely:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 CMG's UCP/EMI 4.0 and 3.5\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 ...\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Full support for MO and MT messages\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _162}{\*\bkmkend _162}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Requirements\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Kannel is being developed on Linux systems, and should be fairly easy to export to other Unix-like systems. However, we don't yet support other platforms, due to lack of time, although it should be working without major problems on Windows (through Cygwin), Solaris and FreeBSD.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel requires the following software environment: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 C compiler and libraries for ANSI C, with normal Unix extensions such as BSD sockets and related tools. (GNU's GCC tool-chain is recommended)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 The Gnome XML library (known as gnome-xml and libxml), version 2.2.5 or newer. See http://xmlsoft.org/xml.html.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 If you are installing it from your distribution's packages, you'll need \fs18\f2 libxml2-dev\fs20\f0  in addition to run-time \fs18\f2 libxml2\fs20\f0  package libraries.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 GNU Make.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 An implementation of POSIX threads (\fs18\f2 pthread.h\fs20\f0 ).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 GNU Bison 1.28, if you want to modify the WMLScript compiler (a pre-generated parser is included for those who just want to compile Kannel).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 DocBook processing tools: DocBook style-sheets, jade, jadetex, etc; see \fs18\f2 README\fs20\f0 , section `Documentation', for more information (pre-formatted versions of the documentation are available, and you can compile Kannel itself even without the documentation tools).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 GNU autoconf, if you want to modify the configuration script.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Hardware requirements are fluffier. Some informal benchmarking have shown that with a reasonably fast PC architecture (e.g. 400MHz Pentium II with 128MB RAM), SMS performance's bottleneck is always on the SMSC side, even for example with multiple connections summing a pipeline with 400 msg/sec. We haven't benchmarked Kannel yet on WAP side, so there are no hard numbers.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 2. Installing the gateway}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 2. Installing the gateway}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _188}{\*\bkmkend _188}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 2. Installing the gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains how the gateway can be installed, either from a source code package or by using a pre-compiled binary version. The goal of this chapter is to get the gateway compiled and all the files in the correct places; the next chapter will explain how the gateway is configured.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl234 \b\fs18\lang1024\f1 Note: \b0 If you are upgrading from a previous version, please look at {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_upgrading_45_notes}{\fldrslt Appendix A}} for any important information.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _194}{\*\bkmkend _194}\b\fs34 Getting the source code\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The source code to Kannel is available for download at http://www.kannel.org/download.shtml. It is available in various formats and you can choose to download either the latest release version or the daily snapshot of the development source tree for the next release version, depending on whether you want to use Kannel for production use or to participate in the development.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you're serious about development, you probably want to use CVS, the version control system used by the Kannel project. This allows you to participate in Kannel development much more easily than by downloading the current daily snapshot and integrating any changes you've made every day. CVS does that for you. (See the Kannel web site for more information on how to use CVS.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _199}{\*\bkmkend _199}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Finding the documentation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The documentation for Kannel consists of three parts: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 1.\tab \i User's Guide\i0 , i.e., the one you're reading at the moment.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 2.\tab \i Architecture and Design\i0 , in \fs18\f2 doc/arch\fs20\f0  or at http://www.kannel.org/arch.shtml (http://www.kannel.org/arch.shtml)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 3.\tab The \fs18\f2 README\fs20\f0  and various other text files in the source tree.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You can also find general information on Kannel's website (http://www.kannel.org) and information about existing problems at our bug tracker (http://bugs.kannel.org).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 We intend to cover everything you need to install and use Kannel is in \i User's Guide\i0 , but the guide is still incomplete in this respect. Similarly, the \i Architecture and Design\i0  document should tell you everything you need to know to dive into the sources and quickly make your own modifications. It's not a replacement for actually reading the source code, but it should work as a map to the source code. The \fs18\f2 README\fs20\f0  is not supposed to be very important, nor contain much information. Instead, it will just point at the other documentation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _221}{\*\bkmkend _221}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Compiling the gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If you are using Kannel on a supported platform, or one that is similar enough to one, compiling Kannel should be trivial. After you have unpacked the source package of your choose, or after you have checked out the source code from CVS, enter the following commands: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        ./configure\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        make\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  The \fs18\f2 configure\fs20\f0  script investigates various things on your computer for the Kannel compilation needs, and writes out the \fs18\f2 Makefile\fs20\f0  used to compile Kannel. \b make\b0  then runs the commands to actually compile Kannel.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If either command writes out an error message and stops before it finishes its job, you have a problem, and you either need to fix it yourself, if you can, or report the problem to the Kannel project. See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_bug_45_reporting}{\fldrslt Chapter 11}} for details.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 For detailed instruction on using the configuration script, see file \fs18\f2 INSTALL\fs20\f0 . That file is a generic documentation for \b configure\b0 . Kannel defines a few additional options: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-defaults=\i type\i0\fs20\f0  Set defaults for the other options. \i\fs18\f2 type\i0\fs20\f0  is either \fs18\f2 speed\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 debug\fs20\f0 . The default is \fs18\f2 speed\fs20\f0 . \fs18\f2 speed\fs20\f0  options is equivalent to \fs18\f2 --with-malloc=native --disable-assertions\fs20\f0 , while \fs18\f2 debug\fs20\f0  is \fs18\f2 --with-malloc=checking --enable-assertions\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --disable-docs (default is --enable-docs)\fs20\f0  Use this option if you don't have DocBook installed and/or you want to save some time and CPU cycles. Pre-generated documentation is available on Kannel's site. Default behavior is to build documentation, b.e., converting the User Guide and the Architecture Guide from the DocBook markup language to PostScript and HTML if DocBook is available.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --enable-drafts (default is --disable-drafts)\fs20\f0  When building documentation, include the sections marked as \fs18\f2 draft\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --enable-debug (default is --disable-debug)\fs20\f0  Enable non-reentrant development time debugging of WMLScript compiler.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --disable-localtime (default is --enable-localtime)\fs20\f0  Write log file time stamps in GMT, not in local time.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --enable-assertions / --disable-assertions\fs20\f0  Turn on or off runtime assertion checking. \fs18\f2 enable\fs20\f0  makes Kannel faster, but gives less information if it crashes. Default value is dependent on \fs18\f2 --with-defaults\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-malloc=\i type\i0\fs20\f0  Select memory allocation module to use: \i\fs18\f2 type\i0\fs20\f0  is \fs18\f2 native\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 checking\fs20\f0 , or \fs18\f2 slow\fs20\f0 . For production use you probably want \fs18\f2 native\fs20\f0 . The \fs18\f2 slow\fs20\f0  module is more thorough than \fs18\f2 checking\fs20\f0 , but much slower. Default value is dependent on \fs18\f2 --with-defaults\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --enable-mutex-stats\fs20\f0  Produce information about lock contention.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --enable-start-stop-daemon\fs20\f0  Compile the start-stop-daemon program.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --enable-pam\fs20\f0  Enable using PAM for authentication of sendsms users for smsbox.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-mssql\i\fs16 [=DIR]\i0\fs20\f0  Enable using FreeTDS libraries for DBPool and DLR support on MS-SQL and Sybase. Optional DIR specifies where to look for FreeTDS libraries and include files (defaults to /usr/local).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-mysql\fs20\f0  Enable using MySQL libraries for DBPool and DLR support.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-mysql-dir=\i DIR\i0\fs20\f0  Where to look for MySQL libs and header files. DIR points to the installation of MySQL.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-sdb\fs20\f0  Enable using LibSDB libraries for dlr support.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-oracle\fs20\f0  Enable using Oracle OCI-Libraries for Oracle 8i/9i DBPool and DLR support.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-oracle-includes=\i DIR\i0\fs20\f0  Where to look for Oracle OCI-Header files.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-oracle-libs=\i DIR\i0\fs20\f0  Where to look for Oracle OCI-Library files.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-redis\fs20\f0  Enable using Redis for DBPool and DLR support. Requires the hiredis library.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-redis-dir=\i DIR\i0\fs20\f0  Where to look for the hiredis library and header files.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-cassandra\fs20\f0  Enable using Cassandra for DBPool and DLR support. Requires the DataStax C/C++ driver for Apache Cassandra.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs18\f2 --with-cassandra-dir=\i DIR\i0\fs20\f0  Where to look for the DataStax C/C++ Cassandra library and header files.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You may need to add compilations flags to configure: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        CFLAGS='-pthread' ./configure\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  The above, for instance, seems to be required on FreeBSD. If you want to develop Kannel, you probably want to add CFLAGS that make your compiler use warning messages. For example, for GCC: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        CFLAGS='-Wall -O2 -g' ./configure\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  (You may, at your preference, use even stricter checking options.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _330}{\*\bkmkend _330}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Installing the gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 After you have compiled Kannel, you need to install certain programs in a suitable place. This is most easily done by using \b make\b0  again: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        make bindir=\i\fs14 /path/to/directory\i0\fs16  install\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  Replace \i\fs18\f2 /path/to/directory\i0\fs20\f0  with the pathname of the actual directory where the programs should be installed. The programs that are installed are (as filenames from the root of the source directory): \sa100\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 gw/bearerbox\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 gw/smsbox\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 gw/wapbox\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033  The version number of the gateway is added to the file names during installation. This makes it easier to have several versions installed, and makes it easy to go back to an older version if the new version proves problematic.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel consists of three programs called boxes: the bearer box is the interface towards the phones. It accepts WAP and SMS messages from the phones and sends them to the other boxes. The SMS box handles SMS gateway functionality, and the WAP box handles WAP gateway functionality. There can be several SMS boxes and several WAP boxes running and they don't have to run on the same host. This makes it possible to handle much larger loads.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _346}{\*\bkmkend _346}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Using pre-compiled binary packages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _348}{\*\bkmkend _348}\fs28 Installing Kannel from RPM packages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains how to install, upgrade and remove Kannel binary RPM packages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Before you install Kannel, check that you have libxml2 installed on your system:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        rpm -q libxml2\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Installing Kannel\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 1. Download the binary RPM packet from the Kannel web site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 2. Install the RPM package: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        rpm -ivh kannel-\i\fs14 VERSION\i0\fs16 .\i\fs14 i386\i0\fs16 .rpm\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 Upgrading Kannel\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 1. Download the binary RPM packet from the Kannel web site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 2. Upgrade the RPM package: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        rpm -Uvh kannel-\i\fs14 VERSION\i0\fs16 .\i\fs14 i386\i0\fs16 .rpm\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 Removing Kannel\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 1. Remove the RPM package: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        rpm -e kannel\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 After you have installed Kannel from the RPM packages you should now be able to run the Kannel init.d script that will start Kannel as a WAP gateway. Run the script as root.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        /etc/rc.d/init.d/kannel start\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To stop the gateway just run the same script with the stop parameter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        /etc/rc.d/init.d/kannel stop\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If Kannel is already running and you just want to quickly stop and start the gateway,e.g.to set a new configuration option, run the script with the restart parameter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        /etc/rc.d/init.d/kannel restart\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If you want Kannel to run as a daemon, you need to add a symbolic link to the Kannel script from the runlevel you want Kannel to run in. E.g. to run Kannel in runlevel 5 add symbolic links to /etc/rc.d/rc5.d/.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        cd /etc/rc.d/rc5.d/\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        ln -s ../init.d/kannel S91kannel\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        ln -s ../init.d/kannel K91kannel\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To run Kannel as a SMS gateway you need to edit the configuration file which is at /etc/kannel/kannel.conf. In Kannel's documentation directory (\fs18\f2 /usr/share/doc/kannel\fs20\f0 ) there is an example file called examples/smskannel.conf. It has some basic examples of the configuration groups needed to run Kannel as a SMS gateway. For more detailed information please see \fs18\f2 examples/kannel.conf\fs20\f0  in the same directory for a commented complete example, and read the section "SMS gateway configuration" later in this same document.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The logging is disabled by default and you can enable it from the kannel.conf file. Just add the log-file option to the group of which box you want to log.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The documentation will be installed at /usr/doc/kannel-VERSION/ or /usr/share/doc/kannel-VERSION/ depending on if you used the RedHat 6.x or a 7.x or newer package.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In the Kannel documentation directory there is a HTML file called control.html. It is an example file that shows how to use the Kannel HTTP administration interface. It also has a template for sending SMS messages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _390}{\*\bkmkend _390}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Installing Kannel from DEB packages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains how to install, upgrade and remove Kannel binary DEB packages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel's packages are available on main Debian repository (http://packages.debian.org/kannel) although that version may be out-of-sync with latest Kannel version.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Before you install Kannel, check that you have libxml2 installed on your system:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        dpkg -l libxml2\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Installing or upgrading Kannel from Debian or Kannel repository\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 1. Install or upgrade the package: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        apt-get install kannel\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 See http://kannel.org/download.shtml#debian_repository for information about kannel repository sources.list\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Installing or upgrading Kannel from a file\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 1. Download the binary DEB packet from the Kannel web site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 2. Log in as root:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        su -\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 3. Install or upgrade the DEB package:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        dpkg -i kannel-\i\fs14 VERSION\i0\fs16 .deb\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Removing Kannel\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 1. Log in as root:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 2. Remove the package keeping configuration files:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        dpkg --remove kannel\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 3. Remove the package completely:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        dpkg --purge kannel\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 After you have installed Kannel from the DEB packages you should now be able to run the Kannel init.d script that will start Kannel as a WAP gateway. Run the script as root.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        /etc/init.d/kannel start\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To stop the gateway just run the same script with the stop parameter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        /etc/init.d/kannel stop\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If Kannel is already running and you just want to quickly stop and start the gateway,e.g.to set a new configuration option, run the script with the restart parameter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        /etc/init.d/kannel restart\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If you don't want Kannel to run as a daemon, run:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        update-rc.d -f kannel remove\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If you want to restore Kannel running as a daemon, you need to add a symbolic link to the Kannel script from the runlevel you want Kannel to run in. E.g. to run Kannel in default runlevel, just run:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        update-rc.d kannel defaults\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Kannel package starts by default with a wapbox daemon. To activate smsbox or select which box you want to start, edit /etc/default/kannel and comment/uncomment START_xxxBOX.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To run Kannel as a SMS gateway you need to edit the configuration file which is at /etc/kannel/kannel.conf. In /usr/share/docs/kannel/examples/ there are example files. They have some basic examples of the configuration groups needed to run Kannel as a SMS gateway. For more detailed information please read the section "SMS gateway configuration" later in this same document.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The documentation will be installed at /usr/share/doc/kannel/.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In the Kannel documentation directory there is a html file called control.html. It is an example file that shows how to use the Kannel HTTP administration interface. It also has a template for sending SMS messages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Additionally to kannel-VERSION.deb, there's now an optional kannel-docs-VERSION.deb with documentation (userguide et al) and a kannel-extras-VERSION.deb with contrib and test stuff.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you want to test development version, use the packages called kannel-devel-*.deb.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 3. Using the gateway}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 3. Using the gateway}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _441}{\*\bkmkend _441}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 3. Using the gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains how the gateway core, bearerbox, is configured and used. It covers the configuration file, keeping an eye on the gateway while it is running, and using the HTTP interface to control the gateway.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 After this chapter there is distinct chapter for each kind of gateway use: WAP gateway, SMS gateway and WAP Push proxy. These chapters explain the configuration and other aspects of gateway of that type.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You can configure your Kannel to be only a WAP or a SMS gateway, or to be both at the same time. You just need to read each chapter and combine all configurations.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 There is only one configuration file for all parts of Kannel, although when Kannel is distributed to several hosts some lines from the configuration file can be removed in some hosts.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _447}{\*\bkmkend _447}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Configuring the gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The configuration file can be divided into three parts: bearerbox configurations, smsbox configurations and wapbox configurations. Bearerbox part has one 'core' group and any used SMS center groups, while wapbox part has only one wapbox group. In smsbox part there is one smsbox group and then number of sms-service and sendsms-user groups.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Details of each part are in an appropriate section of this documentation. The 'core' group used by the bearerbox is explained in this chapter, while 'wapbox' part is in the next chapter and 'smsbox', 'smsc' (SMS center), 'sms-service' and 'sendsms-user' groups are in the SMS Kannel chapter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _451}{\*\bkmkend _451}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Configuration file syntax\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 A configuration file consists of groups of configuration variables. Groups are separated by empty lines, and each variable is defined on its own line. Each group in Kannel configuration is distinguished with a group variable. Comments are lines that begin with a number sign (\fs18\f2 #\fs20\f0 ) and are ignored (they don't, for example, separate groups of variables).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A variable definition line has the name of the variable, and equals sign (\fs18\f2 =\fs20\f0 ) and the value of the variable. The name of the variable can contain any characters except white space and equals. The value of the variable is a string, with or without quotation marks () around it. Quotation marks are needed if the variable needs to begin or end with white space or contain special characters. Normal C escape character syntax works inside quotation marks.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Perhaps an example will make things easier to comprehend: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
1    # A do-nothing service.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
2    group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
3    keyword = nop\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
4    text = "You asked nothing and I did it!"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
5\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
6    # Default service.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
7    group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
8    keyword = default\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
9    text = "No services defined"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  The above snippet defines the keyword \fs18\f2 nop\fs20\f0  for an SMS service, and a default action for situation when the keyword in the SMS message does not match any defined service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Lines 1 and 6 are comment lines. Line 5 separates the two groups. The remaining lines define variables. The group type is defined by the group variable value.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The various variables that are understood in each type of configuration group are explained below.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Some variable values are marked as \fs18\f2 'bool'\fs20\f0 . The value for variable can be like true, false, yes, no, on, off, 0 or 1. Other values are treated as 'true' while if the variable is not present at all, it is treated as being 'false'.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In order to make some configuration lines more readable you may use the delimiter '\\' at the end of a line to wrap and concatenate the next line up to the current line. Here is an example: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
1    # A group with a wrapped alias line\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
2    group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
3    keyword = hello\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
4    aliases = hallo;haalloo;\\\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
5      heelloo;haelloo;healloo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
5    text = "Hello world!"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  The above example shows how a list for various alias keywords is wrapped to two lines using the line wrap delimiter. In order to use the delimiter '\\' itself, you need to escape it via a prefixed '\\' itself. So this is '\\\\' to escape the wrapping function and use the character in the string.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart ID_includes}{\*\bkmkend ID_includes}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Inclusion of configuration files\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 A configuration file may contain a special directive called \fs18\f2 include\fs20\f0  to include other file or a directory with files to the configuration processing.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 This allows to segment the specific configuration groups required for several services and boxes to different files and hence to have more control in larger setups.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is an example that illustrates the \fs18\f2 include \fs20\f0 statement : \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = core\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-port = 13000\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
wapbox-port = 13002\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
wdp-interface-name = "*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-file = "/var/log/bearerbox.log"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-level = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
box-deny-ip = "*.*.*.*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
box-allow-ip = "127.0.0.1"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
include = "wapbox.conf"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
include = "configurations"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Above is the main \fs18\f2 kannel.conf\fs20\f0  configuration file that includes the following \fs18\f2 wapbox.conf\fs20\f0  file with all required directives for the specific box, and a \fs18\f2 configurations\fs20\f0  directory which may include more files to include. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = wapbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
bearerbox-host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-file = "/var/log/wapbox.log"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-level = 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
syslog-level = none\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  The above \fs18\f2 include\fs20\f0  statement may be defined at any point in the configuration file and at any inclusion depth. Hence you can cascade numerous inclusions if necessary.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 At process start time inclusion of configuration files breaks if either the included file can not be opened and processed or the included file has been processed already in the stack and a recursive cycling has been detected.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _481}{\*\bkmkend _481}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Core configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Configuration for Kannel \i MUST\i0  always include a group for general bearerbox configuration. This group is named as 'core' in configuration file, and should be the first group in the configuration file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 As its simplest form, 'core' group looks like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = core\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-port = 13000\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-password = f00bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Naturally this is not sufficient for any real use, as you want to use Kannel as an SMS gateway, or WAP gateway, or both. Thus, one or more of the optional configuration variables are used. In following list (as in any other similar lists), all mandatory variables are marked with \fs18\f2 (m)\fs20\f0 , while conditionally mandatory (variables which must be set in certain cases) are marked with \fs18\f2 (c)\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _489}{\*\bkmkend _489}\b\lang1024 Table 3-1. Core Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 core\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 admin-port (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The port number in which the bearerbox listens to HTTP administration commands. It is NOT the same as the HTTP port of the local HTTP server, just invent any port, but it must be over 1023 unless you are running Kannel as a root process (not recommended) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 admin-port-ssl (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set to true a SSL-enabled administration HTTP server will be used instead of the default insecure plain HTTP server. To access the administration pages you will have to use a HTTP client that is capable of talking to such a server. Use the "https://" scheme to access the secured HTTP server. Defaults to "no". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 admin-password (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password for HTTP administration commands (see below) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 status-password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password to request Kannel status. If not set, no password is required, and if set, either this or \fs18\f2 admin-password\fs20\f0  can be used \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 admin-deny-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 IP-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  These lists can be used to prevent connection from given IP addresses. Each list can have several addresses, separated with semicolons (';'). An asterisk ('*') can be used as a wild-card in a place of any ONE number, so *.*.*.* matches any IP. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 admin-allow-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox-interface (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If this is set, the smsbox listener will only bind to a specified address. For example: "127.0.0.1". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox-port (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is the port number to which the smsboxes, if any, connect. As with admin-port, this can be anything you want. Must be set if you want to handle any SMS traffic. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox-port-ssl (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set to true, the smsbox connection module will be SSL-enabled. Your smsboxes will have to connect using SSL to the bearerbox then. This is used to secure communication between bearerbox and smsboxes in case they are in separate networks operated and the TCP communication is not secured on a lower network layer. Defaults to "no". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wapbox-port (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Like smsbox-port, but for wapbox-connections. If not set, Kannel cannot handle WAP traffic \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wapbox-port-ssl (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set to true, the wapbox connection module will be SSL-enabled. Your wapboxes will have to connect using SSL to the bearerbox then. This is used to secure communication between bearerbox and wapboxes in case they are in separate networks operated and the TCP communication is not secured on a lower network layer. Defaults to "no". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 box-deny-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 IP-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  These lists can be used to prevent box connections from given IP addresses. Each list can have several addresses, separated with semicolons (';'). An asterisk ('*') can be used as a wild-card in place of any ONE number, so *.*.*.* matches any IP. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 box-allow-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 udp-deny-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 IP-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  These lists can be used to prevent UDP packets from given IP addresses, thus preventing unwanted use of the WAP gateway. Used the same way as \fs18\f2 box-deny-ip\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 box-allow-ip\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 udp-allow-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wdp-interface-name (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 IP or '*'\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If this is set, Kannel listens to WAP UDP packets incoming to ports 9200-9208, bound to given IP. If no specific IP is needed, use just an asterisk ('*'). If UDP messages are listened to, wapbox-port variable MUST be set. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A file in which to write a log. This in addition to \fs18\f2 stdout\fs20\f0  and any log file defined in command line. Log-file in 'core' group is only used by the bearerbox. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-level\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number 0..5\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Minimum level of log-file events logged. 0 is for 'debug', 1 'info', 2 'warning, 3 'error' and 4 'panic' (see Command Line Options) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 access-log\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A file in which information about received/sent SMS messages is stored. Access-log in 'core' group is only used by the bearerbox. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 access-log-clean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Indicates if \fs18\f2 access-log\fs20\f0  will contain standard 'markers', which means the 'Log begins', 'Log ends' markers and the prefixed timestamp. This config directive should be set to 'true' if a custom logging format is desired without a prefixed default timestamp. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 access-log-format\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  String defining a custom log file line format. May use escape codes as defined later on to substitute values of the messages into the log entry. If no custom log format is used the standard format will be: \fs18\f2 "%t %l [SMSC:%i] [SVC:%n] [ACT:%A] [BINF:%B] [FID:%F] [META:%D] [from:%p] [to:%P] [flags:%m:%c:%M:%C:%d] [msg:%L:%b] [udh:%U:%u]"\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 syslog-level\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Messages of this log level or higher will also be sent to syslog, the UNIX system log daemon. If not explicitly set with \fs18\f2 syslog-facility\fs20\f0 , it logs under the 'daemon' category. The default is not to use syslog, and you can set that explicitly by setting \fs18\f2 syslog-level\fs20\f0  to 'none'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 syslog-facility\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The syslog facility to send the syslog entries to. The default is 'daemon'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 unified-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  String to unify received phone numbers, for SMSC routing and to ensure that SMS centers can handle them properly. This is applied to 'sender' number when receiving SMS messages from SMS Center and for 'receiver' number when receiving messages from smsbox (either sendsms message or reply to original message). Format is that first comes the unified prefix, then all prefixes which are replaced by the unified prefix, separated with comma (','). For example, for Finland an unified-prefix "+358,00358,0;+,00" should do the trick. If there are several unified prefixes, separate their rules with semicolon (';'), like "+35850,050;+35840,040". \i Note that prefix routing is next to useless now that there are SMSC ID entries. To remove prefixes, use like "-,+35850,050;-,+35840,040". \i0 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  (Deprecated, see \fs18\f2 white-list-sender\fs20\f0 ). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  (Deprecated, see \fs18\f2 white-list-sender-regex\fs20\f0 ). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  (Deprecated, see \fs18\f2 black-list-sender\fs20\f0 ). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  (Deprecated, see \fs18\f2 black-list-sender-regex\fs20\f0 ). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-sender\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Load a list of accepted senders of SMS messages. If a sender of an SMS message is not in this list, any message received from the SMS Center, or tried to be delivered to the SMS Center is rejected. See notes of phone number format from numhash.h header file. NOTE: the system has only a precision of last 9 or 18 digits of phone numbers, so beware! \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-sender-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A regular expression defining the set of accepted senders. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-sender\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As white-list-sender, but SMS messages from these sender numbers are automatically rejected. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-sender-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A regular expression defining the set of rejected senders. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-receiver\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Load a list of accepted receiver of SMS messages. If a receiver of an SMS message is not in this list, any message received from the SMS Center, or tried to be delivered to the SMS Center is rejected. See notes of phone number format from numhash.h header file. NOTE: the system has only a precision of last 9 or 18 digits of phone numbers, so beware! \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-receiver-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A regular expression defining the set of accepted receivers. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-receiver\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As white-list, but SMS messages from these receiver numbers are automatically rejected. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-receiver-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A regular expression defining the set of rejected receivers. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 store-type\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Kannel can use store subsystem that means storing messages on hard disk or in a remote key store until they are successfully handled. By using this subsystem, no SMS messages are lost in Kannel even by crash, but theoretically some messages can duplicate when system is taken down violently. This variable defines a type of backend used for store subsystem. Now two types are supported: a) file: writes store into one single file b) spool: writes store into spool directory (one file for each message) c) redis: writes store into a redis key storage. For redis as message storage you will need a \fs18\f2 group = redis-connection\fs20\f0  and a \fs18\f2 group = store-db\fs20\f0  configuration group that define the connection to the redis server and the table name to be used. See file \fs18\f2 doc/examples/store-redis.conf\fs20\f0  for an example config secttion. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 store-location\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Depends on \fs18\f2 store-type\fs20\f0  option used, it is ether file or spool directory, or none if redis is used as storage subsystem. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 store-dump-freq\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 seconds\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Approximated frequency how often the memory dump of current pending messages are stored to store-file, providing something has happened. Defaults to 10 seconds if not set. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-proxy-host\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 hostname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Enable the use of an HTTP proxy for all HTTP requests. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-proxy-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-proxy-exceptions\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of excluded hosts from being used via a proxy. Separate each entry with space. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-proxy-exceptions-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 UNIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Same as http-proxy-exceptions but match against UNIX regular expression. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-proxy-username\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 username\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Username for authenticating proxy use, for proxies that require this. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-proxy-password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password for authenticating proxy use, for proxies that require this. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-client-certkey-file (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A PEM encoded SSL certificate and private key file to be used with SSL client connections. This certificate is used for the HTTPS client side only, i.e. for SMS service requests to SSL-enabled HTTP servers. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-server-cert-file (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A PEM encoded SSL certificate file to be used with SSL server connections. This certificate is used for the HTTPS server side only, i.e. for the administration HTTP server and the HTTP interface to send SMS messages. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-server-key-file (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A PEM encoded SSL private key file to be used with SSL server connections. This key is associated to the specified certificate and is used for the HTTPS server side only. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-trusted-ca-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This file contains the certificates Kannel is willing to trust when working as a HTTPS client. If this option is not set, certificates are not validated and those the identity of the server is not proven. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-client-cipher-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines the list of encryption suites and ciphers to be used for client side connections. For further details please see https://www.openssl.org/docs/manmaster/man1/ciphers.html \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-server-cipher-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines the list of encryption suites and ciphers to be used for server side connections. For further details please see https://www.openssl.org/docs/manmaster/man1/ciphers.html \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-storage\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 type\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines the way DLRs are stored. Natively in-memory storage is supported, which is very fast, but has no persistancy. This means you will loose all your temporary DLR information on bearebox re-start. In addition a spool directory approach is natively supported, which garantees persistancy with a reasonable performance, depending on your disk IO performance. If you have build-in external DLR storage support, i.e. using MySQL you may define here the alternative storage type like \fs18\f2 mysql\fs20\f0 . Supported types are: \fs18\f2 internal\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 spool\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 mysql\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 pgsql\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 sdb\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 mssql\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 sqlite3\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 oracle\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 redis\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 cassandra\fs20\f0 . By default this is set to \fs18\f2 internal\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-spool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Depends on \fs18\f2 dlr-storage = spool\fs20\f0  option used, it is the spool directory to use for DLR storage data. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 maximum-queue-length\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number of messages\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  (Deprecated, see \fs18\f2 sms-incoming-queue-limit\fs20\f0 ). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sms-incoming-queue-limit\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number of messages\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set maximum size of incoming message queue. After number of messages has hit this value, Kannel began to discard them. Value 0 means giving strict priority to outgoing messages. -1, default, means that the queue of infinite length is accepted. (This works with any normal input, use this variable only when Kannel message queues grow very long). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sms-outgoing-queue-limit\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number of messages\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set maximum size of outgoing message queue. After number of messages has hit this value, Kannel began to discard them. The default value, 1 million, works for most installations. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox-max-pending\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number of messages\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Maximum number of pending messages on the line to smsbox compatible boxes. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sms-resend-freq\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 seconds\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Frequency for the SMS resend thread in which temporarily failed or queued messages will be resent. Defaults to 60 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sms-resend-retry\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Maximum retry attempts for the temporarily failed messages. Defaults to -1, means: unlimited. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sms-combine-concatenated-mo\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Whether Kannel should attempt to combine concatenated MO SMS prior to passing them over to smsbox. Default is true \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sms-combine-concatenated-mo-timeout\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 seconds\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  How long to wait for all concatenated message parts to arrive before timeouting. Default 1800 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-timeout\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 seconds\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Sets socket timeout in seconds for outgoing client http connections. Optional. Defaults to 240 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 A sample more complex 'core' group could be something like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = core\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-port = 13000\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-password = f00bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
status-password = sTat\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-deny-ip = "*.*.*.*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-allow-ip = "127.0.0.1;200.100.0.*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsbox-port = 13003\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
wapbox-port = 13004\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
box-deny-ip = "*.*.*.*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
box-allow-ip = "127.0.0.1;200.100.0.*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
wdp-interface-name = "*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-file = "kannel.log"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-level = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
access-log = "kannel.access"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
unified-prefix = "+358,00358,0;+,00"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
white-list = "http://localhost/whitelist.txt"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _844}{\*\bkmkend _844}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Running Kannel\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To start the gateway, you need to start each box you need. You always need the bearer box, and depending on whether you want WAP and SMS gateways you need to start the WAP and SMS boxes. If you want, you can run several of them, but we'll explain the simple case of only running one each.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _847}{\*\bkmkend _847}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Starting the gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 After you have compiled Kannel and edited configuration file for your taste, you can either run Kannel from command line or use supplied \fs18\f2 start-stop-daemon\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 run_kannel_box\fs20\f0  programs to use it as a daemon service (more documentation about that later).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you cannot or do not know how to set up daemon systems or just want to test Kannel, you probably want to start it from command line. This means that you probably want to have one terminal window for each box you want to start (xterm or screen will do fine). To start the bearerbox, give the following command: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        ./bearerbox -v 1 [config-file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  The \fs18\f2 -v 1\fs20\f0  sets the logging level to \fs18\f2 INFO\fs20\f0 . This way, you won't see a large amount of debugging output (the default is \fs18\f2 DEBUG\fs20\f0 ). Full explanation of Kannel command line arguments is below.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \i [config-file]\i0  is the name of the configuration file you are using with Kannel. The basic distribution packet comes with two sample configuration files, \fs18\f2 smskannel.conf\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 wapkannel.conf\fs20\f0  (in \fs18\f2 gw\fs20\f0  sub directory), of which the first one is for testing out SMS Kannel and the second one for setting up a WAP Kannel. Feel free to edit those configuration files to set up your own specialized system.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 After the bearer box, you can start the WAP box: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        ./wapbox -v 1 [config-file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  or the SMS box: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        ./smsbox -v 1 [config-file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  or both, of course. The order does not matter, except that you need to start the bearer box before the other boxes. Without the bearer box, the other boxes won't even start.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart ID_arguments}{\*\bkmkend ID_arguments}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Command line options\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Bearerbox, smsbox and wapbox each accept certain command line options and arguments when they are launched. These arguments are:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _871}{\*\bkmkend _871}\b\lang1024 Table 3-2. Kannel Command Line Options\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Print the version of the Kannel binary. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --version\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -v <level>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set verbosity level for stdout (screen) logging. Default is 0, which means 'debug'. 1 is 'info, 2 'warning', 3 'error' and 4 'panic' \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --verbosity <level>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -D <places>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set debug-places for 'debug' level output. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --debug <places>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -F <file-name>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Log to file named file-name, too. Does not overrun or affect any log-file defined in configuration file. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --logfile <file-name>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -V <level>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set verbosity level for that extra log-file (default 0, which means 'debug'). Does not affect verbosity level of the log-file defined in configuration file, not verbosity level of the \fs18\f2 stdout\fs20\f0  output. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --fileverbosity <level>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -S\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Start the system initially at SUSPENDED state (see below, bearerbox only) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --suspended\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -I\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Start the system initially at ISOLATED state (see below, bearerbox only) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --isolated\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -H\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Only try to open HTTP sendsms interface; if it fails, only warn about that, do not exit. (smsbox only) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --tryhttp\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -g\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Dump all known config groups and config keys to stdout and exit. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --generate\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -u <username>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Change process user-id to the given. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --user <username>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -p <filename>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Write process PID to the given file. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --pid-file <filename>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -d\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Start process as daemon (detached from a current shell session). Note: Process will change CWD (Current working directory) to \fs18\f2 /\fs20\f0 , therefore you should ensure that all paths to binary/config/config-includes are absolute instead of relative. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --daemonize\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -P\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Start watcher process. This process watch a child process and if child process crashed will restart them automatically. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --parachute\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -X <scriptname>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Execute a given shell script or binary when child process crash detected. This option is usable only with \fs18\f2 --parachute/-P\fs20\f0 . Script will be executed with 2 arguments: scriptname 'processname' 'respawn-count'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 --panic-script <scriptname>\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _976}{\*\bkmkend _976}\b\fs28\f1 Kannel statuses\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 In Kannel, there are four states for the program (which currently directly only apply to bearerbox):\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 a.\tab Running. The gateway accepts, proceeds and relies messages normally. This is the default state for the bearerbox.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab b.\tab Suspended. The gateway does not accept any new messages from SMS centers nor from UDP ports. Neither does it accept new sms and wapbox connections nor sends any messages already in the system onward.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab c.\tab Isolated. In this state, the gateway does not accept any messages from external message providers, which means SMS Centers and UDP ports. It still processes any messages in the system and can accept new messages from sendsms interface in smsbox.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab d.\tab Full. Gateway does not accept any messages from SMS centers, because \fs18\f2 maximum-queue-length\fs20\f0  is achieved.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab e.\tab Shutdown. When the gateway is brought down, it does not accept any new messages from SMS centers and UDP ports, but processes all systems already in the system. As soon as any queues are emptied, the system exits\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 The state can be changed via HTTP administration interface (see below), and shutdown can also be initiated via TERM or INT signal from terminal. In addition, the bearerbox can be started already in suspended or isolated state with -S or -I command line option, see above.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _992}{\*\bkmkend _992}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 HTTP administration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Kannel can be controlled via an HTTP administration interface. All commands are done as normal HTTP queries, so they can be easily done from command line like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        lynx -dump "http://localhost:12345/shutdown?password=bar"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  ...in which the '12345' is the configured admin-port in Kannel configuration file (see above). For most commands, admin-password is required as a argument as shown above. In addition, HTTP administration can be denied from certain IP addresses, as explained in configuration chapter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Note that you can use these commands with WAP terminal, too, but if you use it through the same Kannel, replies to various suspend commands never arrive nor can you restart it via WAP anymore.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _998}{\*\bkmkend _998}\b\lang1024 Table 3-3. Kannel HTTP Administration Commands\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 status or status.txt\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Get the current status of the gateway in a text version. Tells the current state (see above) and total number of messages relied and queuing in the system right now. Also lists the total number of smsbox and wapbox connections. No password required, unless \fs18\f2 status-password\fs20\f0  set, in which case either that or main admin password must be supplied. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 status.html\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  HTML version of status \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 status.xml\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  XML version of status \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 status.wml\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  WML version of status \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 store-status or store-status.txt\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Get the current content of the store queue of the gateway in a text version. No password required, unless \fs18\f2 status-password\fs20\f0  set, in which case either that or main admin password must be supplied. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 store-status.html\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  HTML version of store-status \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 store-status.xml\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  XML version of store-status \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 suspend\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set Kannel state as 'suspended' (see above). Password required. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 isolate\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set Kannel state as 'isolated' (see above). Password required. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 resume\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set Kannel state as 'running' if it is suspended or isolated. Password required. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 shutdown\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Bring down the gateway, by setting state to 'shutdown'. After a shutdown is initiated, there is no other chance to resume normal operation. However, 'status' command still works. Password required. If shutdown is sent for a second time, the gateway is forced down, even if it has still messages in queue. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 flush-dlr\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If Kannel state is 'suspended' this will flush all queued DLR messages in the current storage space. Password required. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 start-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Re-start a single SMSC link. Password required. Additionally the \fs18\f2 smsc\fs20\f0  parameter must be given to identify which \fs18\f2 smsc-admin-id\fs20\f0  should be re-started. The configuration for the SMSC link is re-read from disk before the action is performed. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 stop-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Shutdown a single SMSC link. Password required. Additionally the \fs18\f2 smsc\fs20\f0  parameter must be given (see above). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 add-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Adds an SMSC link previously removed or created after the service was started. Password required. Additionally the \fs18\f2 smsc\fs20\f0  parameter must be given (see above). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 remove-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Removes an existing SMSC link. Password required. Additionally the \fs18\f2 smsc\fs20\f0  parameter must be given (see above). If you want a permanent removal, you should also remove the entry from the configuration file or it will be recreated after a service restart. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 restart\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is the hard restarting version, where we fully stop the bearerbox and then initiate from ground up. Beware that you loose the smsbox connections in such a case. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 graceful-restart\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Re-start bearerbox gracefully, hence only the changes in the configuration for the SMSC connections are performed. I.e. an added SMSC group will be start, and a removed SMSC group will be shutdown and destroyed. Any unchanged SMSC connection keeps on running without interruption. Password required. This is the soft restarting version, where we keep all internal parts of the bearebox running, including the smsbox connections. The same graceful restart can be initiated by sending a SIGHUP signal to the bearerbox daemon process. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-level\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set Kannel log-level of log-files while running. This allows you to change the current log-level of the log-files on the fly. This includes the main event log and the SMSC specific logs, if any exist. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reload-lists\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Re-loads the 'white-list' and 'black-list' URLs provided in the core group. This allows Kannel to keep running while the remote lists change and signal bearerbox to re-load them on the fly. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 remove-message\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Removes the message with the give \fs18\f2 id\fs20\f0  (an UUID) from the local storage subsystem. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 4. Setting up a WAP gateway}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 4. Setting up a WAP gateway}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_wap_45_gateway}{\*\bkmkend ID_wap_45_gateway}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 4. Setting up a WAP gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter tells you how to set Kannel up as a WAP gateway.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1097}{\*\bkmkend _1097}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 WAP gateway configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To set up a WAP Kannel, you have to edit the 'core' group in the configuration file, and define the 'wapbox' group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You must set following variables for the 'core' group: \fs18\f2 wapbox-port\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 wdp-interface-name\fs20\f0 . See previous chapter about details of these variables.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 With standard distribution, a sample configuration file \fs18\f2 wapkannel.conf\fs20\f0  is supplied. You may want to take a look at that when setting up a WAP Kannel.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1105}{\*\bkmkend _1105}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Wapbox group\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If you have set \fs18\f2 wapbox-port\fs20\f0  variable in the 'core' configuration group, you \i MUST\i0  supply a 'wapbox' group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The simplest working 'wapbox' group looks like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = wapbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
bearerbox-host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  There is, however, multiple optional variables for the 'wapbox' group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _1112}{\*\bkmkend _1112}\b\lang1024 Table 4-1. Wapbox Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wapbox\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 bearerbox-host (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 hostname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The machine in which the bearerbox is. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 timer-freq\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 value-in-seconds\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The frequency of how often timers are checked out. Default is 1 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-interface-name\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 IP address\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If this is set then Kannel do outgoing HTTP requests using this interface. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 map-url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL-pair\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The pair is separated with space. Adds a single mapping for the left side URL to the given destination. If you append an asterisk `*' to the left side URL, its prefix Is matched against the incoming URL. Whenever the prefix matches, the URL will be replaced completely by the right side. In addition, if if you append an asterisk to the right side URL, the part of the incoming URL coming after the prefix, will be appended to the right side URL. Thus, for a line: map-url = "http://source/* http://destination/*" and an incoming URL of "http://source/some/path", the result will be "http://destination/some/path" \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 map-url-max\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If you need more than one mapping, set this to the highest number mapping you need. The default gives you 10 mappings, numbered from 0 to 9. Default: 9 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 map-url-0\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL-pair\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Adds a mapping for the left side URL to the given destination URL. Repeat these lines, with 0 replaced by a number up to map-url-max, if you need several mappings. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 device-home\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Adds a mapping for the URL DEVICE:home (as sent by Phone.com browsers) to the given destination URL. There is no default mapping. NOTE: the mapping is added with both asterisks, as described above for the "map-url" setting. Thus, the above example line is equivalent to writing map-url = "DEVICE:home* http://some.where/*" \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As with bearerbox 'core' group. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-level\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number 0..5\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 access-log\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A file containing all requested URLs from clients while wapbox operation, including client IP, HTTP server User-Agent string, HTTP response code, size of reply. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 access-log-clean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Indicates if \fs18\f2 access-log\fs20\f0  will contain standard 'markers', which means the 'Log begins', 'Log ends' markers and the prefixed timestamp. This config directive should be set to 'true' if a custom logging format is desired without a prefixed default timestamp. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 access-log-time\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Determine which timezone we use for access logging. Use 'gmt' for GMT time, anything else will use local time. Default is local time. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 syslog-level\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Messages of this log level or higher will also be sent to syslog, the UNIX system log daemon. If not explicitly set with \fs18\f2 syslog-facility\fs20\f0 , it logs under the 'daemon' category. The default is not to use syslog, and you can set that explicitly by setting \fs18\f2 syslog-level\fs20\f0  to 'none'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 syslog-facility\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The syslog facility to send the syslog entries to. The default is 'daemon'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smart-errors\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set wapbox will return a valid WML deck describing the error that occurred while processing an WSP request. This may be used to have a smarter gateway and let the user know what happened actually. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wml-strict\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set wapbox will use a strict policy in XML parsing the WML deck. If not set it will be more relaxing and let the XML parser recover from parsing errors. This has mainly impacts in how smart the WML deck and it's character set encoding can be adopted, even while you used an encoding that does not fit the XML preamble. BEWARE: This may be a vulnerability in your wapbox for large bogus WML input. Therefore this defaults to 'yes', strict parsing. is assumed. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-timeout\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 seconds\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Sets socket timeout in seconds for outgoing client http connections. Optional. Defaults to 240 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1216}{\*\bkmkend _1216}\b\fs34\f1 Running WAP gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 WAP Gateway is ran as explained in previous chapter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1219}{\*\bkmkend _1219}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Checking whether the WAP gateway is alive\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 You can check whether the WAP gateway (both the bearerbox and the wapbox) is alive by fetching the URL \fs18\f2 kannel:alive\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 5. Setting up wtls security}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 5. Setting up wtls security}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_wtls}{\*\bkmkend ID_wtls}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 5. Setting up wtls security\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter tells you how to set Kannel up to handle wtls traffic.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs18\f2 wtls\fs20\f0  group is optional and single. The prerequisites for this group are to have defined a wapbox group, and a pair of SSL certificates available. Instructions on how to create self-signed 1024-bit RSA certificates are in {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_certificates}{\fldrslt Appendix B}}.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Current imlementation provides for the following functionality:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab A.\tab Supported MACs:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 SHA_0\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 SHA_40\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 SHA_80\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 SHA_NOLIMIT\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 MD5_40\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 MD5_80\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 MD5_NOLIMIT\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Missing:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 SHA_XOR_40\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab B.\tab Supported Ciphers:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RC5_CBC_40\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RC5_CBC_56\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RC5_CBC\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 DES_CBC\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 DES_CBC_40\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Missing:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 NULL_bulk\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 TRIPLE_DES_CBC_EDE\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 IDEA_CBC_40\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 IDEA_CBC_56\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 IDEA_CBC\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab C.\tab Supported Keys:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RSA_anon\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Missing:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RSA_anon_512\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RSA_anon_768\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RSA_NOLIMIT\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RSA_512\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 RSA_768\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 ECDH_anon\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 ECDH_anon_113\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 ECDH_anon_131\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 ECDH_ECDSA_NOLIMIT\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Keys might seem a shortcoming, but all mobiles support RSA_anon. Some of the other RSA_anon keys (i.e. RSA_anon_512, RSA_anon_768) are propably supported as well, just haven't been tested yet.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab D.\tab All wtls states except:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Suspend/Resume wtls session\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1520\sl260\fi-200 \tx1520 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Cipher change when already connected. In practice this is handled through another client hello, while already connected to the same client\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The simplest working \fs18\f2 wtls\fs20\f0  group looks like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = wtls\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
certificate-file = /etc/kannel/server.crt\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
privatekey-file = /etc/kannel/server.key\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Can also be the same single combined pem file with both certificate and privatekey parts. The complete variable list for the \fs18\f2 wtls\fs20\f0  group is:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1315}{\*\bkmkend _1315}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Wtls configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _1317}{\*\bkmkend _1317}\fs20\f0 Table 5-1. Wtls Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wtls\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 certificate-file (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Public key SSL certificate. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 privatekey-file (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Private key SSL certificate. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 privatekey-password (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 Phrase\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Needed only if private key was created with a passphrase. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 6. Setting up MSISDN provisioning for WAP gateway}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 6. Setting up MSISDN provisioning for WAP gateway}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_msisdn_45_provisioning}{\*\bkmkend ID_msisdn_45_provisioning}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 6. Setting up MSISDN provisioning for WAP gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter tells you how to set Kannel up to deliver the MSISDN number of the WAP device using the WAP gateway.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Mostly this feature is interested because the WAP protocol stack itself does not provide such a protocol layer bridging of information. In case you want to know which unique MSISDN is used by the WAP device your HTTP application is currently interacting, then you can pick this information from a RADIUS server that is used by a NAS (network access server) for authentication and accounting purposes.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel provides a RADIUS accounting proxy thread inside wapbox which holds a mapping table for the dynamically assigned (DHCP) IP addresses of the WAP clients and their MSISDN numbers provided to the NAS device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Beware that you \i HAVE TO\i0  to be in control of the NAS to configure it to use Kannel's wapbox as RADIUS accounting server. You can not use MSISDN provisioning via Kannel's RADIUS accounting proxy if you can not forward the accounting packets to Kannel's accounting proxy thread. So obviously this feature is for operators and people who have dedicated dial-in servers (NAS).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1357}{\*\bkmkend _1357}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 RADIUS accounting proxy configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To set up the RADIUS accounting proxy thread inside Kannel you have to define a 'radius-acct' group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1360}{\*\bkmkend _1360}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 RADIUS-Acct configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The simplest working 'radius-acct' group looks like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = radius-acct\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
our-port = 1646\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
secret-nas = radius\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  This example does not forward any accounting packets to a remote RADIUS server. There is, however, multiple optional variables for the 'radius-acct' group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _1364}{\*\bkmkend _1364}\b\lang1024 Table 6-1. RADIUS-Acct Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 radius-acct\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is mandatory variable if you want to have Kannel's RADIUS accounting proxy thread to be active inside wapbox. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 secret-nas (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Specifies the shared secret between NAS and our RADIUS accounting proxy. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 secret-radius\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Specifies the shared secret between our RADIUS accounting proxy and the remote RADIUS server itself in case we are forwarding packets. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 our-host\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 IP address\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Specifies the local interface where our-port value will bind to. (defaults to 0.0.0.0) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 remote-host\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 FQDN or IP address\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Specifies the remote RADIUS server to which we forward the accounting packets. If none is given, then no forwarding of accounting packets is performed. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 our-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Specifies the port to which our RADIUS accounting proxy thread will listen to. (defaults according to RFC2866 to 1813) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 remote-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Specifies the port to which our RADIUS accounting proxy thread will forward any packets to the remote-host. (defaults according to RFC2866 to 1813) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 remote-timeout\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Specifies the timeout value in milliseconds for waiting on a response from the remote RADIUS server. (defaults to 40 msecs.) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 nas-ports\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Specifies how many possible clients can connect simultaneously to the NAS. This value is used to initialize the mapping hash table. If does not require to be exact, because the table grows automatically if required. (defaults to 30) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 unified-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  String to unify provided phone numbers from NAS. Format is that first comes the unified prefix, then all prefixes which are replaced by the unified prefix, separated with comma (','). For example, for Finland an unified-prefix "+358,00358,0;+,00" should do the trick. If there are several unified prefixes, separate their rules with semicolon (';'), like "+35850,050;+35840,040". \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1433}{\*\bkmkend _1433}\b\fs28\f1 Using the MSISDN provisioning on HTTP server side\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Kannel's wapbox will include a specific HTTP header in the HTTP request issued to the URL provided by the WAP client. That HTTP header is \fs18\f2 X-WAP-Network-Client-MSISDN\fs20\f0 . It will carry as value the MSISDN of the WAP client if the RADIUS accounting proxy has received a valid accounting packet from the NAS that provided the client IP.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 7. Setting up a SMS Gateway}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 7. Setting up a SMS Gateway}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_sms_45_gateway}{\*\bkmkend ID_sms_45_gateway}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 7. Setting up a SMS Gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter is a more detailed guide on how to set up Kannel as an SMS gateway.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1440}{\*\bkmkend _1440}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Required components\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To set up an SMS gateway, you need, in addition to a machine running Kannel, access to (an operator's) SMS center, or possibly to multiple ones. The list of supported SMS centers and their configuration variables is below.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you do not have such access, you can still use Kannel as an SMS gateway via \i phone-as-SMSC\i0  feature, by using a GSM phone as a virtual SMS center.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition to an SMS center (real or virtual), you need some server to handle any SMS requests received. This server then has simple or more complex cgi-bins, programs or scripts to serve HTTP requests generated by Kannel in response to received SMS messages. These services can also initiate SMS push via Kannel smsbox HTTP sendsms interface.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1446}{\*\bkmkend _1446}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 SMS gateway configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To set up a SMS Kannel, you have to edit the 'core' group in the configuration file, and define an 'smsbox' group plus one or more 'sms-service' groups, plus possibly one or more 'sendsms-user' groups.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 For the 'core' group, you must set the following variable: \fs18\f2 smsbox-port\fs20\f0 . In addition, you may be interested to set \fs18\f2 unified-prefix\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 white-list\fs20\f0  and/or \fs18\f2 black-list\fs20\f0  variables. See above for details of these variables.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A sample configuration file \fs18\f2 smskannel.conf\fs20\f0  is supplied with the standard distribution. You may want to take a look at that when setting up an SMS Kannel.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1456}{\*\bkmkend _1456}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 SMS centers\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To set up the SMS center at Kannel, you have to add a 'smsc' group into configuration file. This group must include all the data needed to connect that SMS center. You may also want to define an ID (identification) name for the SMSC, for logging and routing purposes.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 SMSC ID is an abstract name for the connection. It can be anything you like, but you should avoid any special characters. You do not need to use ID, but rely on SMS center IP address and other information. However, if you use the ID, you do not need to re-define sms-services nor routing systems if the IP of the SMS Center is changed, for example.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Common 'smsc' group variables are defined in the following table. The first two (\fs18\f2 group\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 smsc\fs20\f0 ) are mandatory, but rest can be used if needed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _1463}{\*\bkmkend _1463}\b\lang1024 Table 7-1. SMSC Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Identifies the SMS center type. See below for a complete list. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An optional name or id for the smsc. Any string is acceptable, but semicolon ';' may cause problems, so avoid it and any other special non-alphabet characters. This 'id' is written into log files and can be used to route SMS messages, and to specify the used SMS-service. Several SMSCs can have the same id. The name is case-insensitive. Note that if SMS Center connection has an assigned SMSC ID, it does NOT automatically mean that messages with identical SMSC ID are routed to it; instead configuration variables \fs18\f2 denied-smsc-id\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 allowed-smsc-id\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 preferred-smsc-id\fs20\f0  is used for that. If you want to use Delivery Reports, you must define this. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-admin-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An optional id for the smsc to be used on administrative commands. This allows commands targeted to individual binds even if they share the same \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  (for load balancing scenarios, for example). Any string is acceptable, but semicolon ';' may cause problems, so avoid it and any other special non-alphabet characters. Several SMSCs can have the same admin-id, though it's not recommended. The name is case-insensitive. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 throughput\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 float (messages/sec)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If SMSC requires that Kannel limits the number of messages per second, use this variable. This is considered as active throttling. (optional) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-smsc-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 id-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  SMS messages with SMSC ID equal to any of the IDs in this list are never routed to this SMSC. Multiple entries are separated with semicolons (';') \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-smsc-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 id-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This list is opposite to previous: only SMS messages with SMSC ID in this list are ever routed to this SMSC. Multiple entries are separated with semicolons (';') \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 preferred-smsc-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 id-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  SMS messages with SMSC ID from this list are sent to this SMSC instead than to SMSC without that ID as preferred. Multiple entries are separated with semicolons (';') \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of phone number prefixes which are accepted to be sent through this SMSC. Multiple entries are separated with semicolon (';'). For example, "040;050" prevents sending of any SMS message with prefix of 040 or 050 through this SMSC. If denied-prefix is unset, only these numbers are allowed. If set, number are allowed if present in allowed or not in denied list. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of phone number prefixes which are NOT accepted to be sent through this SMSC. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 preferred-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As \fs18\f2 denied-prefix\fs20\f0 , but SMS messages with receiver starting with any of these prefixes are preferably sent through this SMSC. In a case of multiple preferences, one is selected at random (also if there are preferences, SMSC is selected randomly) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 unified-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  String to unify received phone numbers, for SMSC routing and to ensure that SMS centers can handle them properly. This is applied to 'sender' number when receiving SMS messages from SMS Center and for 'receiver' number when receiving messages from smsbox (either sendsms message or reply to original message). Format is that first comes the unified prefix, then all prefixes which are replaced by the unified prefix, separated with comma (','). For example, for Finland an unified-prefix "+358,00358,0;+,00" should do the trick. If there are several unified prefixes, separate their rules with semicolon (';'), like "+35850,050;+35840,040". \i Note that prefix routing is next to useless now that there are SMSC ID entries. To remove prefixes, use like "-,+35850,050;-,+35840,040". \i0 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-charset\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As some SMS Centers do not follow the standards in character coding, an \fs18\f2 alt-charset\fs20\f0  character conversion is presented. This directive acts different for specific SMSC types. Please see your SMSC module type you want to use for more details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-dcs\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional data coding scheme value usage. Sets the 'alt-dcs' value for the sendsms HTTP interface to a fixed value for each SMS sent via this SMSC connection. If equals to yes, uses FX. If equals to no (default), uses 0X. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 our-host\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 hostname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional hostname or IP address in which to bind the connection in our end. TCP/IP connection only. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A file in which to write a log of the given smsc output. Hence this allows to log smsc specific entries to a separate file. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-level\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number 0..5\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Minimum level of log-file events logged. 0 is for 'debug', 1 'info', 2 'warning, 3 'error' and 4 'panic' (see Command Line Options) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reconnect-delay\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Number of seconds to wait between single re-connection attempts. Hence all SMSC modules should use this value for their re-connect behavior. (Defaults to '10' seconds). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reroute\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set for a smsc group, all inbound messages coming from this smsc connection are passed internally to the outbound routing functions. Hence this messages is not delivered to any connected box for processing. It is passed to the bearerbox routing as if it would have originated from an externally connected smsbox. (Defaults to 'no'). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reroute-smsc-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Similar to 'reroute'. Defines the explicit smsc-id the MO message should be passed to for MT traffic. Hence all messages coming from the the configuration group smsc are passed to the outbound queue of the specified smsc-id. This allows direct proxying of messages between 2 smsc connections without injecting them to the general routing procedure in bearerbox. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reroute-receiver\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Similar to 'reroute'. Defines the explicit smsc-id routes for specific receiver numbers of messages that are coming from this smsc connection. Format is that first comes the smsc-id to route the message to, then all receiver numbers that should be routed, separated with comma (','). For example, route receivers 111 and 222 to smsc-id A and 333 and 444 to smsc-id B would look like: "A,111,222; B,333,444". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reroute-dlr\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Indicate whether DLR's should be re-routed too, if one of above reroute rules are enabled. Please note, that SMSC-Module should support DLR sending. At time of writing none of SMSC-Module supports DLR sending. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-smsc-id-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  SMS messages with SMSC ID equal to any of the IDs in this set of SMSC IDs are always routed to this SMSC. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-smsc-id-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  SMS messages with SMSC ID equal to any of the IDs in this set of SMSC IDs are never routed to this SMSC. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 preferred-smsc-id-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  SMS messages with SMSC ID in this set of SMSC IDs are sent to this SMSC as preferred. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A set of phone number prefixes which are accepted to be sent through this SMSC. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A set of phone number prefixes which may not be sent through this SMSC. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 preferred-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As \fs18\f2 denied-prefix-regex\fs20\f0 , but SMS messages with receiver matching any of these prefixes are preferably sent through this SMSC. In a case of multiple preferences, one is selected at random. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 max-sms-octets\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Maximum allowed octets for this SMSC. If this maximum exceeds Kannel will split SMS into multiparts. Default: 140 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dead-start\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, defines if the SMSC connection type is started in disconnected mode (dead). This allows SMSC to be connected at a later time with the add-smsc HTTP administration command. If set to 'true' the connection will start as 'dead' and will require a start-smsc command to activate it. Defaults to 'false' if not present.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 instances\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The number of same instances of this group to be created. This allows to declare the config group one time in the configuration and to multiply it by this value for the numner of runtime instances. (default: 1) \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 In addition to these common variables there are several variables used by certain SMS center connections. Each currently supported SMS center type is explained below, with configuration group for each. Note that many of them use variables with same name, but most also have some specific variables.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \i NOTE: SMS center configuration variables are a bit incomplete, and will be updated as soon as people responsible for the protocols are contacted. Meanwhile, please have patience.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1664}{\*\bkmkend _1664}\b\i0\fs28\lang1024\f1 Nokia CIMD 1.37 and 2.0\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Support for CIMD 1.37 is quite old and will be removed in a future version of Kannel. Please let us know if you still need it.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = cimd\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
host = 100.101.102.103\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
port = 600\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 The driver for CIMD2 is a "receiving SME" and expects the SMSC to be configured for that. It also expects the SMSC to automatically send stored messages as soon as Kannel logs in (this is the normal configuration).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = cimd2\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
host = 100.101.102.103\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
port = 600\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keepalive = 5 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
my-number = "12345"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 host (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 hostname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Machine that runs the SMSC. As IP (100.100.100.100) or hostname (their.machine.here) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Port number in the smsc host machine \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-username (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Username in the SMSC machine/connection account \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-password (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password in the SMSC machine needed to contact SMSC \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 keepalive\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  SMSC connection will not be left idle for longer than this many seconds. The right value to use depends on how eager the SMSC is to close idle connections. If you see many unexplained reconnects, try lowering this value. Set it to 0 to disable this feature. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 no-dlr\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. If defined, status delivery report requests (DLR) won't be requested at all. Some CIMD2 SMSC have prohibited these reports so if you are getting error like "Incorrect status report request parameter usage", this option is for you. Defaults to "false". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 my-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The number that the SMSC will add in front of the sender number of all messages sent from Kannel. If Kannel is asked to send a message, it will remove this prefix from the sender number so that the SMSC will add it again. If the prefix was not present, Kannel will log a warning and will not send the sender number. If \fs18\f2 my-number\fs20\f0  is not set, or is set to \fs18\f2 "never"\fs20\f0 , then Kannel will not send the sender number to the SMSC at all. If you want Kannel to pass all sender numbers to the SMSC unchanged, then just set \fs18\f2 sender-prefix\fs20\f0  to the empty string \fs18\f2 ""\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 our-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional port number in which to bind the connection in our end. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1730}{\*\bkmkend _1730}\b\fs28\f1 CMG UCP/EMI 4.0 and 3.5\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp100\li1600\ri200\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp0\li1600\ri200\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\sl374\qc Warning\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp100\li1600\ri200\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp0\li1600\ri200\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\sl234 \b0\fs18\lang1033 See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_upgrading_45_notes}{\fldrslt Appendix A}} for changes.\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp100\li1600\ri200\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp0\li1600\ri200\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\sl-100\keepn\par\pard\sl-1\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Kannel supports two types of connections with CMG SMS centers: direct TCP/IP connections ( \fs18\f2 emi\fs20\f0 ) and ISDN/modem (X.25) connection (\fs18\f2 emi_x25\fs20\f0 ).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl234 \b\fs18\lang1024\f1 Note: \b0\fs16\f2 emi_x25\fs18\f1  is an old implementation and supports less features than it's IP counterpart. If you still need this module, please tell us to \fs16\f2 devel@kannel.org\fs18\f1  so we can fix it.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Sample configurations for these are:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = emi\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
host = 103.102.101.100\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
port = 6000\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keepalive = 55\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
our-port = 600 (optional bind in our end)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
receive-port = 700 (the port in which the SMSC will contact)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
idle-timeout = 30\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = emi_x25\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
phone = ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
device = /dev/tty0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 host (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 hostname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Machine that runs SMSC. As IP (100.100.100.100) or hostname (their.machine.here) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Port number in the SMSC host machine \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-host\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 hostname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional alternate Machine that runs SMSC. As IP (100.100.100.100) or hostname (their.machine.here) (If undef but exists alt-port, emi2 would try host:alt-port) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional alternate Port number in the SMSC host machine (If undef but exists alt-host, emi2 would try alt-host:port) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-username\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Username in the SMSC machine/connection account \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password in the SMSC machine needed to contact SMSC \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 device (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 device-name\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The device the modem is connected to, like \fs18\f2 /dev/ttyS0\fs20\f0 . ISDN connection only. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 phone (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Phone number to dial to, when connecting over a modem to an SMS center. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 our-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional port number in which to bind the connection in our end. TCP/IP connection only. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 our-receiver-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional port number allowing to specify our port when connecting an SMSC. TCP/IP connection only. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 receive-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional port number we listen to and to which the SMS center connects when it has messages to send. Required if SMS center needs one connection to send and other to receive. TCP/IP connection only. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 appname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Name of a "Send only" service. Defaults to \fs18\f2 send\fs20\f0 . All outgoing messages are routed through this service. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 connect-allow-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 IP-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, only connections from these IP addresses are accepted to receive-port. TCP/IP connection only. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 idle-timeout\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (seconds)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If this option is set to a value larger than 0, then the connection will be closed after the configured amount of seconds without activity. This option interacts with the \fs18\f2 keepalive\fs20\f0  configuration option. If \fs18\f2 keepalive\fs20\f0  is smaller than \fs18\f2 idle-timeout\fs20\f0 , then the connection will never be idle and those this option has no effect. If \fs18\f2 keepalive\fs20\f0  is larger than \fs18\f2 idle-timeout\fs20\f0 , than \fs18\f2 keepalive\fs20\f0  reopens the connection. This allows one to poll for pending mobile originated Short Messages at the SMSC. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 keepalive\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (seconds)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A keepalive command will be sent to the SMSC connection this many seconds after the last message. The right value to use depends on how eager the SMSC is to close idle connections. 50 seconds is a good guess. If you see many unexplained reconnects, try lowering this value. Set it to 0 to disable this feature. Requires username or my-number to be set. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wait-ack\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (seconds)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A message is resent if the acknowledge from SMSC takes more than this time. Defaults to 60 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wait-ack-expire\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines what kind of action should be taken if the the ack of a message expires. The options for this value are: 0x00 - disconnect/reconnect, (default) 0x01 - as is now, re-queue, but this could potentially result in the msg arriving twice 0x02 - just carry on waiting (given that the wait-ack should never expire this is the must accurate) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 flow-control\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This SMSC can support two types of flow control. The first type of flow control is a \fs18\f2 stop-and-wait\fs20\f0  protocol, when this parameter equals to '1'. During the handling of commands, no other commands shall be sent before the a response is received. Any command that is sent before the reception of the response will be discarded. The second type of flow control is \fs18\f2 windowing\fs20\f0 , when this parameter is unset or equals '0'. In this case a maximum of n commands can be sent before a response is received. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 window\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (messages)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  When using \fs18\f2 flow-control=0\fs20\f0 , emi works in windowed flow control mode. This variable defines the size of the window used to send messages. (optional, defaults to the maximum - 100) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 my-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If the large account number is different from the short number, assign it with this variable. For example, if short number is 12345 and large account is 0100100100101234 (IP+port), set my-number to 12345 and every message received will have that receiver. In addition, if you are bound to the SMSC without an explicit login, use this configuration directive to enable keep-alive (OP/31) operations. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-charset\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines which character conversion kludge may be used for this specific link. Currently implemented alternative charsets are defined in "alt_charsets.h" and new ones can be added. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 notification-pid\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 4 num char.\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Notification PID value. See below for a complete list and other notes. Example: 0539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_notification_45_pid_45_note}{\*\bkmkend ID_notification_45_pid_45_note}\up8\fs12 a\up0\fs20  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 notification-addr\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string (max 16)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Notification Address. Example: 0100110120131234. {\*\bkmkstart ID_notification_45_addr_45_note}{\*\bkmkend ID_notification_45_addr_45_note}\up8\fs12 b\up0\fs20  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li60\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Notes:\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 a. \tab If you set \fs18\f2 notification-pid\fs20\f0 , you should also set \fs18\f2 notification-addr\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 b. \tab If you set \fs18\f2 notification-addr\fs20\f0 , you should also set \fs18\f2 notification-pid\fs20\f0 .\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 You should use \fs18\f2 notification-pid\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 notification-addr\fs20\f0  if you need to inform your exact "address" to your smsc. For example, if you have a \fs18\f2 Multiple-Address (MA)\fs20\f0  account with several connections to the same \fs18\f2 short number\fs20\f0 , you may need to tell your smsc to send delivery reports to the exact instance that sent the message. This is required because if you send a message with instance 1, your instance 2 wouldn't know about it, unless you use a shared DB store for delivery reports.\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Notification PID Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0100\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Mobile Station\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0122\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Fax Group 3\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0131\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 X.400\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0138\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Menu over PSTN\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0139\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 PC appl. over PSTN (E.164)\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0339\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 PC appl. over X.25 (X.121)\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0439\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 PC appl. over ISDN (E.164)\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0539\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 PC appl. over TCP/IP\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1945}{\*\bkmkend _1945}\b\fs28\f1 SMPP 3.3, 3.4 and 5.0\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This implements Short Message Peer to Peer (SMPP) Protocol 5.0, with most used support for 3.4 and full support for 3.3. Sample configuration:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = smpp\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
host = 123.123.123.123\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
port = 600\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-username = "STT"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-password = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
system-type = "VMA"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
address-range = ""\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 host (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 hostname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Machine that runs SMSC. As IP (100.100.100.100) or hostname (their.machine.here) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The port number for the TRANSMITTER connection to the SMSC. If set, receive-port must be ommited or set to 0. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 use-ssl\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines whether we should try to bind with SSL enabled connection to create a SSMPP (secure SMPP) session. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-client-certkey-file (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A PEM encoded SSL certificate and private key file to be used for SSL connections. This option is used together with use-ssl for client certificate validation with SMPP SMSCs requiring it. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 transceiver-mode\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use a TRANSCEIVER mode connection to the SMSC. It uses the standard transmit 'port' to define the port to connect to. This is a SMPP 3.4 and 5.0 feature and will not work on an earlier SMSC using SMPP 3.3. This will do a bind_transceiver only and will not attempt to fall back to doing transmit and receive on the same connection. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 receive-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The port number for the RECEIVER connection to the SMSC. If set, port must be ommited or set to 0. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-username (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The 'username' of the Messaging Entity connecting to the SMSC. If the SMSC operator reports that the "TELEPATH SYSTEM MANAGER TERMINAL" view "Control.Apps.View" value "Name:" is "SMPP_ZAPVMA_T" for the transmitter and "SMPP_ZAPVMA_R" for the receiver the smsc-username value is accordingly "SMPP_ZAP". Note that this used to be called system-id (the name in SMPP documentation) and has been changed to smsc-username to make all Kannel SMS center drivers use the same name. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-password (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The password matching the "smsc-username" your teleoperator provided you with. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 system-type (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional; used to categorize the type of ESME that is binding to the SMSC. Examples include "VMS" (voice mail system) and "OTA" (over-the-air activation system). If not set, defaults to "VMA" (voice mail alerting). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 service-type\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional; if specified, sets the service type for the SMSC. If unset, the default service type is used. This may be used to influence SMS routing (for example). The SMSC operator may also refer to this as the "profile ID". The maximum length of the service type is 6, according to the SMPP specification v3.4. Defined values are: "CMT" (cellular messaging), "CPT" (cellular paging), "VMN" (voice mail notification), "VMA" (voice mail alerting), "WAP" (wireless application protocol), "USSD" (unstructured supplementary services data), "CBS" (cell broadcast service) and "GUTS" (generic UDP transport service). Other values may be defined mutually between the SMSC and the ESME application. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 interface-version\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Change the "interface version" parameter sent from Kannel to a value other then 0x34 (for SMPP v3.4). The value entered here should be the hexadecimal representation of the interface version parameter. For example, the default (if not set) is "34" which stands for 0x34. For SMPP v3.3 set to "33". For SMPP v5.0 set to "50". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 address-range (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  According to the SMPP 3.4 spec this is supposed to affect which MS's can send messages to this account. Doesn't seem to work, though. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 my-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional smsc short number. Should be set if smsc sends a different one. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 enquire-link-interval\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional the time lapse allowed between operations after which an SMPP entity should interrogate whether it's peer still has an active session. The default is 30 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 max-pending-submits\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional the maximum number of outstanding (i.e. acknowledged) SMPP operations between an ESME and SMSC. This number is not specified explicitly in the SMPP Protocol Specification and will be governed by the SMPP implementation on the SMSC. As a guideline it is recommended that no more than 10 (default) SMPP messages are outstanding at any time. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reconnect-delay\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional the time between attempts to connect an ESME to an SMSC having failed to connect initiating or during an SMPP session. The default is 10 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 source-addr-ton\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, source address TON setting for the link. (Defaults to 2). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 source-addr-npi\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, source address NPI setting for the link. (Defaults to 1). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 source-addr-autodetect\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, if defined tries to scan the source address and set TON and NPI settings accordingly. If you don't want to autodetect the source address, turn this off, by setting it to no. (Defaults to yes). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dest-addr-ton\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, destination address TON setting for the link. (Defaults to 2). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dest-addr-npi\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, destination address NPI setting for the link. (Defaults to 1). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 bind-addr-ton\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, bind address TON setting for the link. (Defaults to 0). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 bind-addr-npi\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, bind address NPI setting for the link. (Defaults to 0). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 msg-id-type\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional, specifies which number base the SMSC is using for the message ID numbers in the corresponding \fs18\f2 submit_sm_resp\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 deliver_sm\fs20\f0  PDUs. This is required to make delivery reports (DLR) work on SMSC that behave differently. The number is a combined set of bit 1 and bit 2 that indicate as follows: bit 1: type for \fs18\f2 submit_sm_resp\fs20\f0 , bit 2: type for \fs18\f2 deliver_sm\fs20\f0 . If the bit is set then the value is in hex otherwise in decimal number base. Which means the following combinations are possible and valid: 0x00 \fs18\f2 deliver_sm\fs20\f0  decimal, \fs18\f2 submit_sm_resp\fs20\f0  decimal; 0x01 \fs18\f2 deliver_sm\fs20\f0  decimal, \fs18\f2 submit_sm_resp\fs20\f0  hex; 0x02 \fs18\f2 deliver_sm\fs20\f0  hex, \fs18\f2 submit_sm_resp\fs20\f0  decimal; 0x03 \fs18\f2 deliver_sm\fs20\f0  hex, \fs18\f2 submit_sm_resp\fs20\f0  hex. In accordance to the SMPP v3.4 specs the default will be a C string literal if no of the above values is explicitly indicated using the config directive. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-charset\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines which character encoding is used for this specific smsc link. Uses \fs18\f2 iconv()\fs20\f0  routines to convert from and to that specific character set encoding. See your local iconv_open(3) manual page for the supported character encodings and the type strings that should be presented for this directive. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-addr-charset\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines which character encoding is used for alphanumeric addresses. When set to \fs18\f2 GSM\fs20\f0 , addresses are converted into the GSM 03.38 charset (Since @ is translated into 0x00 which will break the SMPP PDU, @ replaced with ?). If set to another value, \fs18\f2 iconv()\fs20\f0  is used. (Defaults to windows-1252) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 connection-timeout\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (seconds)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This timer specifies the maximum time lapse allowed between transactions , after which period of inactivity, an SMPP driver may assume that the session is no longer active and does reconnect. Defaults to 300 seconds, to disable set it to 0. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wait-ack\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (seconds)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A message is resent if the acknowledge from SMSC takes more than this time. Defaults to 60 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wait-ack-expire\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines what kind of action should be taken if the ack of a message expires. The options for this value are: 0x00 - disconnect/reconnect, (default) 0x01 - as is now, re-queue, but this could potentially result in the msg arriving twice 0x02 - just carry on waiting (given that the wait-ack should never expire this is the must accurate) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 validityperiod\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  How long the message will be valid, i.e., how long the SMSC will try try to send the message to the recipient. Defined in minutes. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 esm-class\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Change the "esm_class" parameter sent from Kannel. Accepted values are 0 (Default SMSC Mode) and 3 (Store and Forward). Defaults to 3. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-format\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Selects which SMPP PDU dump format to use: 1 - for multiline (default) 2 - for one line \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _2162}{\*\bkmkend _2162}\b\fs28\f1 Sema Group SMS2000 OIS 4.0, 5.0 and 5.8\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The 4.0 implementation is over Radio PAD (X.28). Following configuration variables are needed, and if you find out the more exact meaning, please send a report.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The 5.0 implementation uses X.25 access gateway.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The 5.8 implementation uses direct TCP/IP access interface.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = sema\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
device = /dev/tty0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc_nua = (X121 smsc address)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
home_nua = (x121 radio pad address)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
wait_report = 0/1 (0 means false, 1 means true)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 device (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 device\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  ex: /dev/tty0 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc_nua (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X121 smsc address\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The address of an SMSC for SEMA SMS2000 protocols using an X.28 connection. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 home_nua (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X121 radio pad address\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The address of a radio PAD implementing Sema SMS2000 using X.28 connection. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wait_report\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 0 (false)/1 (true)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Report indicator used by the Sema SMS2000 protocol. Optional. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = ois\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
host = 103.102.101.100\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
port = 10000\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
