0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Delivery reports are supported by default internally, which means all DLRs are stored in the memory of the bearerbox process. This is problematic if bearerbox crashes or you take the process down in a controlled way, but there are still DLRs open. Therefore you may use external DLR storage places, i.e. a MySQL database.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Following are the supported DLR storage types and how to use them:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3542}{\*\bkmkend _3542}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Internal DLR storage\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This is the default way in handling DLRs and does not require any special configuration. In order to configure bearerbox to use internal DLR storage use \fs18\f2 dlr-storage = internal\fs20\f0  in the \fs18\f2 core\fs20\f0  group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3547}{\*\bkmkend _3547}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 MySQL DLR storage\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To store DLR information into a MySQL database you may use the \fs18\f2 dlr-storage = mysql\fs20\f0  configuration directive in the \fs18\f2 core\fs20\f0  group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition to that you must have a \fs18\f2 dlr-db\fs20\f0  group defined that specifies the table field names that are used to the DLR attributes and a \fs18\f2 mysql-connection\fs20\f0  group that defines the connection to the MySQL server itself.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is the example configuration from \fs18\f2 doc/examples/dlr-mysql.conf\fs20\f0 : \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
