\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _3558}{\*\bkmkend _3558}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 MySQL connection configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 For several reasons external storage may be required to handle dynamical issues, i.e. DLRs, sms-service, sendsms-user, ota-setting, ota-bookmark definitions and so on. To define a MySQL database connection you simple need to specify a \fs18\f2 mysql-connection\fs20\f0  group as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _3562}{\*\bkmkend _3562}\b\lang1024 Table 7-4. MySQL Connection Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 mysql-connection\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 id (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An optional name or id to identify this MySQL connection for internal reference with other MySQL related configuration groups. Any string is acceptable, but semicolon ';' may cause problems, so avoid it and any other special non-alphabet characters. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 host (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 hostname or IP\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Hostname or IP of a server running a MySQL database to connect to. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 username (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 username\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  User name for connecting to MySQL database. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 password (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password for connecting to MySQL database. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 database (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Name of database in MySQL database server to connect to. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 max-connections\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  How many connections should be opened to the given database. This is used for database pool. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 A sample 'mysql-connection' group: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = mysql-connection\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
id = dlr-db\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
database = dlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-connections = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 In case you use different MySQL connections for several storage issues, i.e. one for DLR and another different one for sms-service you may use the \fs18\f2 include\fs20\f0  configuration statement to extract the MySQL related configuration groups to a separate \fs18\f2 mysql.conf \fs20\f0 file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3613}{\*\bkmkend _3613}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 LibSDB DLR storage\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To store DLR information into a LibSDB resource (which is an abstraction of a real database) you may use the \fs18\f2 dlr-storage = sdb\fs20\f0  configuration directive in the \fs18\f2 core\fs20\f0  group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition to that you must have a \fs18\f2 dlr-db\fs20\f0  group defined that specifies the table field names that are used to the DLR attributes and a \fs18\f2 sdb-connection\fs20\f0  group that defines the LibSDB resource itself.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is the example configuration from \fs18\f2 doc/examples/dlr-sdb.conf\fs20\f0  using a PostgreSQL resource: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sdb-connection\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
id = pgdlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
url = "postgres:host=localhost:db=myapp:port=1234"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-connections = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = dlr-db\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
id = pgdlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
table = dlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-smsc = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-timestamp = ts\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-destination = destination\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-source = source\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-service = service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-url = url\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-mask = mask\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-status = status\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-boxc-id = boxc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Beware that you have the DB support build in your LibSDB installation when trying to use a specific DB type within the URL.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3625}{\*\bkmkend _3625}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Oracle 8i/9i DLR storage\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To store DLR information into a Oracle database you may use the \fs18\f2 dlr-storage = oracle\fs20\f0  configuration directive in the \fs18\f2 core\fs20\f0  group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition to that you must have a \fs18\f2 dlr-db\fs20\f0  group defined that specifies the table field names that are used to the DLR attributes and a \fs18\f2 oracle-connection\fs20\f0  group that defines the connection to the Oracle server itself.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is the example configuration from \fs18\f2 doc/examples/dlr-oracle.conf\fs20\f0 : \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = oracle-connection\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
id = mydlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
tnsname = dlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-connections = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
