\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3670}{\*\bkmkend _3670}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Redis DLR storage\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To store DLR information using the Redis (http://www.redis.io) keystore, you should set the \fs18\f2 dlr-storage = redis\fs20\f0  configuration directive in the \fs18\f2 core\fs20\f0  group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition, you must have a \fs18\f2 dlr-db\fs20\f0  group defined that specifies the key and hash field names that are used to store the DLR attributes and a \fs18\f2 redis-connection\fs20\f0  group that defines the connection to the Redis server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Since Redis is a keystore and not a SQL database, keys and not tables are used to store DLR attributes. For ease of configuration, the same configuration parameters in the \fs18\f2 dlr-db\fs20\f0  group that are used to define SQL tables in other DLR storage engines are used to configure key names in Redis. DLRs are stored as Redis hash keys with a separate key for each DLR. Key names are in the format \fs18\f2 <table>:<smsc>:<timestamp>\fs20\f0 . Some SMSCs also append the destination to the DLR keyname resulting DLR keynames in the format \fs18\f2 <table>:<smsc>:<timestamp>:<destination>\fs20\f0 . You can specify the \fs18\f2 <table>\fs20\f0  portion of the keyname by specifying the \fs18\f2 table\fs20\f0  value in the \fs18\f2 dlr-db\fs20\f0  group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition to one key per DLR, an additional key with a name in the format \fs18\f2 <table>:Count\fs20\f0  is created to maintain a count of the total number of outstanding DLRs.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is the example configuration from \fs18\f2 doc/examples/dlr-redis.conf\fs20\f0 : \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                group = redis-connection\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                id = mydlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                port = 6379\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                #password = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                database = 2\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                idle-timeout = 3600\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                max-connections = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                group = dlr-db\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                id = mydlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ttl = 604800\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                table = dlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-smsc = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-timestamp = ts\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-destination = destination\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-source = source\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-service = service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-url = url\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-mask = mask\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-status = status\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-boxc-id = boxc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _3690}{\*\bkmkend _3690}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Redis connection configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To define a Redis connection you need to specify a \fs18\f2 redis-connection\fs20\f0  group as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _3694}{\*\bkmkend _3694}\b\lang1024 Table 7-5. Redis Connection Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 redis-connection\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 id (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An optional name or id to identify this Redis connection for internal reference with other Redis related configuration groups. Any string is acceptable, but semicolon ';' may cause problems, so avoid it and any other special non-alphabet characters. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 host (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 hostname or IP\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Hostname or IP of a server running Redis to connect to. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If the Redis server is secured with a password in redis.conf, it must be specified here. By default, Redis servers are unsecured and in that case, this parameter may be ommitted.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 database\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The numeric ID (0-15) of the Redis database to use. Ideally this database should be dedicated to storing Redis DLRs since a separate key wil be created for each DLR. If this variable is ommitted, the default database (0) will be used. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 idle-timeout\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The number of seconds that Redis should hold the connection open without any traffic. Some Redis servers are configured to disconnect idle clients very quickly, and if this is the case you should set this value to something more reasonable. If ommitted, the Redis server-wide timeout from redis.conf will apply. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 max-connections\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  How many connections should be opened to the Redis server. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3740}{\*\bkmkend _3740}\b\fs28\f1 Cassandra DLR storage\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To store DLR information using Cassandra (http://cassandra.apache.org/) database, you should set the \fs18\f2 dlr-storage = cassandra\fs20\f0  configuration directive in the \fs18\f2 core\fs20\f0  group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In addition, you must have a \fs18\f2 dlr-db\fs20\f0  group defined that specifies the field names that are used to store the DLR attributes and a \fs18\f2 cassandra-connection\fs20\f0  group that defines the connection to Cassandra.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is the example configuration from \fs18\f2 doc/examples/dlr-cassandra.conf\fs20\f0 : \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                group = cassandra-connection\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                id = mydlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                #username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                #password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                #database = dlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                max-connections = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                group = dlr-db\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                id = mydlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ttl = 604800\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                table = dlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-smsc = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-timestamp = ts\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-destination = destination\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-source = source\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-service = service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-url = url\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-mask = mask\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-status = status\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                field-boxc-id = boxc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3751}{\*\bkmkend _3751}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 DLR database field configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 For external database storage of DLR information in relational database management systems (RDBMS) you will have to specify which table field are used to represent the stored data. This is done via the \fs18\f2 dlr-db\fs20\f0  group as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _3755}{\*\bkmkend _3755}\b\lang1024 Table 7-6. DLR Database Field Configuration Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-db\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 id (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An id to identify which external connection should be used for DLR storage. Any string is acceptable, but semicolon ';' may cause problems, so avoid it and any other special non-alphabet characters. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 table (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The name of the table that is used to store the DLR information. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-smsc (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used for the smsc data. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-timestamp (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used for the timestamp data. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-destination (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used for the destination number data. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-source (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used for the source number data. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-service (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used for the service username data. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-url (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used for the DLR URL which is triggered when the DLR for this message arrives from the SMSC. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-mask (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used for the DLR mask that has been set for a message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-status (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used to reflect the status of the DLR for a specific message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 field-boxc-id (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The table field that is used to store the smsbox connection id that has passed the message for delivery. This is required in cases you want to guarantee that DLR messages are routed back to the same smsbox conn instance. This is done via the smsbox routing. If you don't use smsbox routing simply add this field to your database table and keep it empty. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 A sample 'dlr-db' group: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = dlr-db\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
id = dlr-db\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
table = dlr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-smsc = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-timestamp = ts\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-source = source\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-destination = destination\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-service = service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-url = url\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-mask = mask\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-status = status\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
field-boxc-id = boxc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Beware that all variables in this group are mandatory, so you have to specify all fields to enable bearerbox to know how to store and retrieve the DLR information from the external storage spaces.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _3829}{\*\bkmkend _3829}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 SMSBox configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3831}{\*\bkmkend _3831}\fs28 Smsbox group\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 You must define an 'smsbox' group into the configuration file to be able to use SMS Kannel. The simplest working 'smsbox' group looks like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
bearerbox-host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  ...but you would most probably want to define 'sendsms-port' to be able to use SMS push.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 SMSBox inherits from core the following fields: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsbox-port\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http-proxy-port\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http-proxy-host\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http-proxy-username\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http-proxy-password\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http-proxy-exceptions\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http-proxy-exceptions-regex\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ssl-certkey-file\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _3837}{\*\bkmkend _3837}\b\fs20\lang1024\f0 Table 7-7. Smsbox Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 bearerbox-host (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 hostname\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The machine in which the bearerbox is. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 bearerbox-port (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is the port number to which smsbox will connect bearerbox. If not given \fs18\f2 smsbox-port\fs20\f0  from core group used. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 bearerbox-port-ssl (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set to true, the smsbox connection will be SSL-enabled. Your smsbox will connect using SSL to the bearerbox then. This is used to secure communication between bearerbox and smsboxes in case they are in separate networks operated and the TCP communication is not secured on a lower network layer. If not given \fs18\f2 smsbox-port-ssl\fs20\f0  from core group used. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox-id (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional smsbox instance identifier. This is used to identify an smsbox connected to an bearerbox for the purpose of having smsbox specific routing inside bearerbox. So if you you own boxes that do pass messages into bearerbox for delivery you may want that answers to those are routed back to your specific smsbox instance, i.e. SMPP or EMI proxying boxes. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sendsms-interface (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If this is set, the sendsms HTTP interface will only bind to a specified address. For example: "127.0.0.1". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sendsms-port (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 port-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The port in which any sendsms HTTP requests are done. As with other ports in Kannel, can be set as anything desired. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sendsms-port-ssl (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set to true, the sendsms HTTP interface will use a SSL-enabled HTTP server with the specified ssl-server-cert-file and ssl-server-key-file from the core group. Defaults to "no". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sendsms-url (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  URL locating the sendsms service. Defaults to \fs18\f2 /cgi-bin/sendsms\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sendota-url (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  URL locating the sendota service. Defaults to \fs18\f2 /cgi-bin/sendota\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 immediate-sendsms-reply (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a backward compatibility flag: when set, Kannel will immediately answer to any sendsms requests, without knowing if the bearerbox will ever accept the message. If set to false (default), smsbox will not reply to HTTP request until the bearerbox has received the message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sendsms-chars\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Only these characters are allowed in 'to' field when send-SMS service is requested via HTTP. Naturally, you should allow at least \fs18\f2 0123456789\fs20\f0 . The \i space\i0  character (' ') has special meaning: it is used to separate multiple phone numbers from each other in multi-send. To disable this feature, do not have it as an accepted character. If this variable is not set, the default set \fs18\f2 "0123456789\~+-"\fs20\f0  is used. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 global-sender\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 phone-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, all sendsms originators are set as these before proceeding. Note that in a case of most SMS centers you cannot set the sender number, but it is automatically set as the number of SMSC \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As with the bearerbox 'core' group. Access-log is used to store information about MO and send-sms requests. Can be named same as the 'main' access-log (in 'core' group). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 log-level\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number 0..5\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 access-log\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 syslog-level\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Messages of this log level or higher will also be sent to syslog, the UNIX system log daemon. If not explicitly set with \fs18\f2 syslog-facility\fs20\f0 , it logs under the 'daemon' category. The default is not to use syslog, and you can set that explicitly by setting \fs18\f2 syslog-level\fs20\f0  to 'none'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 syslog-facility\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The syslog facility to send the syslog entries to. The default is 'daemon'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Load a list of accepted destinations of SMS messages. If a destination of an SMS message is not in this list, any message received from the HTTP interface is rejected. See notes of phone number format from numhash.h header file. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As white-list, but SMS messages to these numbers are automatically discarded \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reply-couldnotfetch\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, replaces the SMS message sent back to user when Kannel could not fetch content. Defaults to \fs18\f2 Could not fetch content, sorry.\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reply-couldnotrepresent\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, replaces the SMS message sent back when Kannel could not represent the result as a SMS message. Defaults to \fs18\f2 Result could not be represented as an SMS message.\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reply-requestfailed\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, replaces the SMS message sent back when Kannel could not contact http service. Defaults to \fs18\f2 Request Failed\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 reply-emptymessage\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, replaces the SMS message sent back when message is empty. Set to "" to enable empty messages. Defaults to \fs18\f2 <Empty reply from service provider>\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 mo-recode\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If enabled, Kannel will try to convert received messages with UCS-2 charset to WINDOWS-1252 or to UTF-8, simplifying external servers jobs. If Kannel is able to recode message, it will also change \fs18\f2 coding\fs20\f0  to \fs18\f2 7 bits\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 charset\fs20\f0  to \fs18\f2 windows-1252\fs20\f0  or to \fs18\f2 utf-8\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-request-retry\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, specifies how many retries should be performed for failing HTTP requests of sms-services. Defaults to 0, which means no retries should be performed and hence no HTTP request queuing is done. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-queue-delay\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, specifies how many seconds should pass within the HTTP queuing thread for retrying a failed HTTP request. Defaults to 10 sec. and is only obeyed if \fs18\f2 http-request-retry\fs20\f0  is set to a non-zero value. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines the set of accepted destinations of SMS messages. If a destination of an SMS message is not in this set, any message received from the HTTP interface is rejected. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As white-list-regex, but SMS messages to numbers within in this set are automatically discarded. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 max-pending-requests\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number of messages\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Maximum number of pending MO or DLR messages that are handled in parallel. (Default: 512) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 http-timeout\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 seconds\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Sets socket timeout in seconds for outgoing client http connections. Optional. Defaults to 240 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sms-length\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Maximum allowed number of characters for a single SMS in smsbox. If this maximum exceeds Kannel will split SMS into multiparts. Default: 140 \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 A typical 'smsbox' group could be something like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
bearerbox-host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
sendsms-port = 13131\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
sendsms-chars = "0123456789 "\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
global-sender = 123456\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
access-log = "kannel.access"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-file = "smsbox.log"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-level = 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _4029}{\*\bkmkend _4029}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Smsbox routing inside bearerbox\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The communication link between bearerbox and smsbox has been designed for the purpose of load-balancing via random assignment. Which means, bearerbox holds all smsc connections and passes inbound message to one of the connected smsboxes. So you have a determined route for outbound messages, but no determined route for inbound messages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The smsbox routing solves this for the inbound direction. In certain scenarios you want that bearerbox to know to which smsbox instance it should pass messages. I.e. if you implement own boxes that pass messages to bearerbox and expect to receive messages defined on certain rules, like receiver number or smsc-id. This is the case for EMI/UCP and SMPP proxies that can be written easily using smsbox routing facility.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you box handles the SMPP server specific communication to your EMSEs, and if an client send a submit_sm PDU, the box would transform the message into Kannel message representation and inject the message to bearerbox as if it would be an smsbox. As you want to assign your clients shortcodes for certain networks or route any inbound traffic from a certain smsc link connected to bearerbox, you need to separate in the scope of bearerbox where the inbound message will be going to. An example may look like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsbox-id = mysmsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsbox-route\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsbox-id = mysmsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
shortcode = "1111;2222;3333"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Which means any inbound message with receiver number 1111, 2222 or 3333 will be delivered to the smsbox instance that has identified itself via the id "mysmsc" to bearerbox. Using this routing the smsbox instance (which may be an EMI/UCP or SMPP proxy) is able to send a deliver_sm PDU. Another example showing the combination use of criteria smsc-id and shortcode looks like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsbox-id = mysmsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsbox-route\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsbox-id = mysmsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-id = "A;B;C"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
shortcode = "1111;2222"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Which means any inbound message with receiver number 1111 or 2222 that have been originating from the smsc-id connections A, B or C will be delivered to the via the id "mysmsc" to bearerbox.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 smsbox-route inherits from core the following fields:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _4037}{\*\bkmkend _4037}\b\lang1024 Table 7-8. Smsbox-route Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox-route\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox-id (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines for which smsbox instance the routing rules do apply. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 word-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, specifies from which smsc-ids all inbound messages should be routed to this smsbox instance. List contains smsc-ids separated by semicolon (";"). This rule may be used to pull any smsc specific message stream to an smsbox instance. If used in combination with config directive shortcode, then this is another matching criteria for the routing decission. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 shortcode\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, specifies which receiver numbers for inbound messages should be routed to this smsbox instance. List contains numbers separated by semicolon (";"). This rule may be used to pull receiver number specific message streams to an smsbox instance. If used in combination with config directive smsc-id, then only messages originating from the connections in the smsc-id are matched against the shortcode list. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _4068}{\*\bkmkend _4068}\b\fs28\f1 SMS-service configurations\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Now that you have an SMS center connection to send and receive SMS messages you need to define services for incoming messages. This is done via 'sms-service' configuration groups.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 These groups define SMS services in the smsbox, so they are only used by the smsbox. Each service is recognized from the first word in an SMS message and by the number of arguments accepted by the service configuration (unless \fs18\f2 catch-all\fs20\f0  configuration variable is used). By adding a username and password in the URL in the following manner "http://luser:password@host.domain:port/path?query" we can perform HTTP Basic authentication.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The simplest service group looks like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keyword = www\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
get-url = "http://%S"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  This service grabs any SMS with two words and 'www' as the first word, and then does an HTTP request to an URL which is taken from the rest of the message. Any result is sent back to the phone (or requester), but is truncated to the 160 characters that will fit into an SMS message, naturally.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Service group \fs18\f2 default\fs20\f0  has a special meaning: if the incoming message is not routed to any other service, \fs18\f2 default\fs20\f0  'sms-service' group is used. You should always define \fs18\f2 default\fs20\f0  service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Service group \fs18\f2 black-list\fs20\f0  has a special meaning: if the incoming message is in service's black-list, this service is used to reply to user. If unset, message will be discarded.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _4081}{\*\bkmkend _4081}\b\lang1024 Table 7-9. SMS-Service Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sms-service\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 keyword (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 word\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Services are identified by the first word in the SMS. Each `%s' in the URL corresponds to one word in the SMS message. Words are separated with spaces. A keyword is matched only if the number of words in the SMS message is the same as the number of `%s' fields in the URL. This allows you to configure the gateway to use different URLs for the same keyword depending on the number of words the SMS message contains. The keyword is case insensitive, which means you don't have to use aliases to handle different cased versions of your keyword. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 keyword-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This field may be used to enable service-selection based on a regular expression. You can define either keyword or keyword-regex in configuration, but not both in the same sms-service. keyword-regex is case sensitive. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 aliases\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 word-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If the service has aliases, they are listed as a list with each entry separated with a semicolon (';'). Aliases are case insensitive just like keyword. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 name\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional name to identify the service in logs. If unset, \fs18\f2 keyword\fs20\f0  is used. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 get-url (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Requested URL. The url can include a list of parameters, which are parsed before the url is fetched. See below for these parameters. Also works with plain 'url' \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 post-url (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Requested URL. As above, but request is done as POST, not GET. Always matches the keyword, regardless of pattern matching. See notes on POST other where. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 post-xml (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Requested URL. As above, but request is done as XML POST. Always matches the keyword, regardless of pattern matching. See notes on POST other where and {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_postxml_46_title}{\fldrslt \i XML Post}} \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 file (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  File read from a local disc. Use this variable only if no \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  is set. All escape codes (parameters) in \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  are supported in filename. The last character of the file (usually linefeed) is removed. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 text (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Predefined text answer. Only if there is neither \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  nor \fs18\f2 file\fs20\f0  set. Escape codes (parameters) are usable here, too. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 exec (c)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Executes the given shell command as the current UID of the running smsbox user and returns the output to \fs18\f2 stdout\fs20\f0  as reply. Escape codes (parameters) are usable here, too. BEWARE: You may harm your system if you use this sms-service type without serious caution! Make sure anyone who is allowed to use these kind of services is checked using white/black-list mechanisms for security reasons. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 accepted-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 id-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Accept ONLY SMS messages arriving from SMSC with matching ID. \up8\fs12 a\up0\fs20  Separate multiple entries with ';'. For example, if \fs18\f2 accepted-smsc\fs20\f0  is "RL;SON", accept messages which originate from SMSC with ID set as 'RL' or 'SON' \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 accepted-account\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 id-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Accept ONLY SMS messages arriving with a matching ACCOUNT field. \up8\fs12 b\up0\fs20  Separate multiple entries with ';'. For example, if \fs18\f2 accepted-account\fs20\f0  is "FOO;BAR", accept messages which originate with the ACCOUNT field set as 'FOO' or 'BAR' \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of phone number prefixes of the sender number which are accepted to be received by this service. \up8\fs12 c\up0\fs20  Multiple entries are separated with semicolon (';'). For example, "91;93" selects this service for these prefixes. If denied-prefix is unset, only this numbers are allowed. If denied is set, number are allowed if present in allowed or not in denied list. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of phone number prefixes of the sender number which are NOT accepted to be sent through this SMSC. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-receiver-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of phone number prefixes of the receiver number which are accepted to be received by this service. This may be used to allow only inbound SMS to certain shortcut numbers to be allowed to this service. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-receiver-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of phone number prefixes of the receiver number which are NOT accepted to be sent through this SMSC. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 catch-all\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Catch keyword regardless of '%s' parameters in pattern. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 send-sender\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Used only with POST. If set to true, number of the handset is set, otherwise not. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 strip-keyword\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Used only with POST. Remove matched keyword from message text before sending it onward. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 faked-sender\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 phone-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This number is set as sender. Most SMS centers ignore this, and use their fixed number instead. This option overrides all other sender setting methods. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 max-messages\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If the message to be sent is longer than maximum length of an SMS it will be split into several parts. \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  lets you specify a maximum number of individual SMS messages that can be used. If \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  is set to 0, no reply is sent, except for error messages. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 accept-x-kannel-headers\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Request reply can include special X-Kannel headers but these are only accepted if this variable is set to true. See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_xkannel_46_title}{\fldrslt \i Extended headers}}. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 assume-plain-text\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If client does not set Content-Type for reply, it is normally application/octet-stream which is then handled as data in Kannel. This can be forced to be plain/text to allow backward compatibility, when data was not expected. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 concatenation\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Long messages can be sent as independent SMS messages with \fs18\f2 concatenation = false\fs20\f0  or as concatenated messages with \fs18\f2 concatenation = true\fs20\f0 . Concatenated messages are reassembled into one long message by the receiving device. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 split-chars\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Allowed characters to split the message into several messages. So, with "#!" the message is split from last '#' or '!', which is included in the previous part. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 split-suffix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If the message is split into several ones, this string is appended to each message except the last one. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 omit-empty\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Normally, Kannel sends a warning to the user if there was an empty reply from the service provider. If \fs18\f2 omit-empty\fs20\f0  is set to 'true', Kannel will send nothing at all in such a case. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 header\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If specified, this string is automatically added to each SMS sent with this service. If the message is split, it is added to each part. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 footer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As header, but not inserted into head but appended to end. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Stuff in answer that is cut away, only things between prefix and suffix is left. Not case sensitive. Matches the first prefix and then the first suffix. These are only used for \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  type services, and only if both are specified. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 suffix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Load a list of accepted senders of SMS messages. If a sender of an SMS message is not in this list, any message received from the SMSC is rejected, unless a \fs18\f2 black-list\fs20\f0  service is defined. See notes of phone number format from numhash.h header file. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As white-list, but SMS messages from these numbers are automatically discarded \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 accepted-smsc-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Accept only SMS messages arriving from SMSCs with a matching ID. \up8\fs12 d\up0\fs20  See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 accepted-account-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Accept ONLY SMS messages arriving with a matching ACCOUNT field. \up8\fs12 e\up0\fs20  See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A set of phone number prefixes of sender-numbers accepted by this service. \up8\fs12 f\up0\fs20  See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A set of phone number prefixes of sender-numbers which may not use this service. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-receiver-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A set of phone number prefixes of receiver-numbers which may receive data sent by this service. This can be used to allow only inbound SMS to certain shortcut numbers to be allowed to this service. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-receiver-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A set of phone number prefixes of receiver-numbers which may not receive data sent by this service. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines a set of accepted senders of SMS messages. If a sender of an SMS message is not in this list, the message is rejected. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As white-list-regex, but SMS messages from these numbers are automatically discarded. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-charset\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines which character encoding is used for the SMS message when passed to a remote HTTP application. This includes how the SMS message text is send in the HTTP GET parameter list and in the HTTP POST bidy. Uses \fs18\f2 iconv()\fs20\f0  routines to convert from and to that specific character set encoding. See your local \fs18\f2 iconv_open(3)\fs20\f0  manual page for the supported character encodings and the type strings that should be presented for this directive. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string (URL)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional Defines a default URL which is fetched for DLR event, if no specific X-Kannel HTTP header parameter is returned by the HTTP response. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-mask\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number (bit mask)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Defines a default DLR event bit mask which is used in combination with the \fs18\f2 dlr-url\fs20\f0  config directive, if no specific X-Kannel HTTP header parameter is returned by the HTTP response. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li60\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Notes:\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 a. \tab Even if this service is denied, Kannel still searches for other service which accepts the message, or default service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 b. \tab Even if this service is denied, Kannel still searches for other service which accepts the message, or default service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 c. \tab Like in accepted-smsc, Kannel still searches for other service which accepts the message. This way there could be several services with the same keyword and different results.\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 d. \tab Even if this service is denied, Kannel still searches for other service which accepts the message, or default service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 e. \tab Even if this service is denied, Kannel still searches for other service which accepts the message, or default service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 f. \tab Like in accepted-smsc-regex, Kannel still searches for another service which accepts the message. This way there could be several services with the same keyword and different results.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart ID_escapecodes}{\*\bkmkend ID_escapecodes}\b\fs20 Table 7-10. Parameters (Escape Codes)\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %k\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the keyword in the SMS request (i.e., the first word in the SMS message) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %s\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  next word from the SMS message, starting with the second one (i.e., the first word, the keyword, is not included); problematic characters for URLs are encoded (e.g., '+' becomes '%2B') \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %S\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  same as %s, but '*' is converted to '~' (useful when user enters a URL) and URL encoding isn't done (all others do URL encode) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %r\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  words not yet used by %s; e.g., if the message is "FOO BAR FOOBAR BAZ", and the has been one %s, %r will mean "FOOBAR BAZ" \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %a\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  all words of the SMS message, including the first one, with spaces squeezed to one \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %b\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the original SMS message, in a binary form (URL-encoded) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %e\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the original SMS message, in a binary form (printable hexadecimal byte codes). I.e. "xyz" would be send as byte codes "78797A". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %t\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the time the message was sent, formatted as "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM", e.g., "1999-09-21 14:18" \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %T\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the time the message was sent, in UNIX epoch timestamp format \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %p\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the phone number of the sender of the SMS message \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %P\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the phone number of the receiver of the SMS message \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %q\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  like %p, but a leading `00' is replaced with `+' \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %Q\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  like %P, but a leading `00' is replaced with `+' \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %i\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the smsc-id of the connection that received the message \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %I\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the SMS ID of the internal message structure \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %d\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the delivery report value \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %R\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the delivery report URL value \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %N\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the delivery report notification message \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %D\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  meta-data from the SMSC. See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_meta_45_data}{\fldrslt Meta Data}} for more into. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %A\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the delivery report SMSC reply, if any \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %F\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the \fs18\f2 foreign\fs20\f0  (smsc-provided) message ID. Only relevant on DLR url's. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %n\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the sendsms-user or sms-service name \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %c\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  message coding: 0 (default, 7 bits), 1 (8 bits) or 2 (Unicode) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %m\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  message class bits of DCS: 0 (directly to display, flash), 1 (to mobile), 2 (to SIM) or 3 (to SIM toolkit). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %M\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  mwi (message waiting indicator) bits of DCS: 0 (voice), 1, (fax), 2 (email) or 3 (other) for activation and 4, 5, 6, 7 for deactivation respectively. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %C\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  message charset: for a "normal" message, it will be "GSM" (coding=0), "binary" (coding=1) or "UTF-16BE" (coding=2). If the message was successfully recoded from Unicode, it will be "WINDOWS-1252" \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %u\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 udh\fs20\f0  of incoming message \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %B\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  billing identifier/information of incoming message. The value depends on the SMSC module and the associated billing semantics of the specific SMSC providing the information. For EMI2 the value is the XSer 0c field, for SMPP MO it is the service_type of the deliver_sm PDU, and for SMPP DLR it is the DLR message err component. (Note: This is used for proxying billing information to external applications. There is no semantics associated while processing these.) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %o\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  account identifier/information of incoming message. The value depends on the SMSC module and has been introduced to allow the forwarding of an operator ID from aggregator SMSCs to the application layer, hence the smsbox HTTP calling instance. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %O\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  DCS (Data coding schema) value. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %f\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Originating SMSC of incoming message. The value is set if the AT driver is used to receive a SMS on a gsm modem. The value of %f will contain the number of the SMSC sending the SMS to the SIM card. Other SMSC types than AT do not set this field so it will be empty. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %x\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  the smsbox-id of the message, identifying from which smsbox connection the message is originating. (Only available withing the \fs18\f2 access-log-format\fs20\f0  directive) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %v\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Validity period in minutes. Available only if SMSC supports and sent this value. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %V\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Deferred in minutes. Available only if SMSC supports and sent this value. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %#\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This allows to pass meta-data individual parameters into urls. The syntax is as follows: \fs18\f2 %#group#parameter#\fs20\f0  For example: \fs18\f2 %#smpp#my_param#\fs20\f0  would be replaced with the value 'my_param' from the group 'smpp' coming inside the meta_data field. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Some sample 'sms-service' groups: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keyword = nop\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
text = "You asked nothing and I did it!"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
catch-all = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keyword = complex\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
get-url = "http://host/service?sender=%p&text=%r"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
accept-x-kannel-headers = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-messages = 3\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
concatenation = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keyword = default\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
text = "No action specified"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _4512}{\*\bkmkend _4512}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 How sms-service interprets the HTTP response\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 When an \fs18\f2 sms-service\fs20\f0  requests a document via HTTP, it will accept one of four types of content types: \sa200\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 text/plain\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Blanks are squeezed into one, rest is chopped to fit an SMS message.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 text/html\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Tags are removed, rest is chopped to fit an SMS message.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 text/vnd.wap.wml\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Processed like HTML.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 text/xml\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Processed as a POST-XML. See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_postxml_46_title}{\fldrslt \i XML Post}}\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 application/octet-stream\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 The body will be transmitted as the SMS message, as 8-bit data. This can be avoided by setting \fs18\f2 assume-plain-text\fs20\f0  variable on for the SMS-service.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart ID_xkannel}{\*\bkmkend ID_xkannel}\b\f1 Extended headers\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Kannel uses and accepts several X-Kannel headers to be used with SMS-services, if option \fs18\f2 accept-x-kannel-headers\fs20\f0  was provided in the relevant 'sms-service' group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _4545}{\*\bkmkend _4545}\b\lang1024 Table 7-11. X-Kannel Headers\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 SMSPush equivalent\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 X-Kannel Header\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 username\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Username\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Password\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 from\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-From\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 to\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-To\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 text\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 request body\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 charset\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 charset as in \fs18\f2 Content-Type: text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 udh\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-UDH\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-SMSC\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 flash\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Flash\fs20\f0  (deprecated, see \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-MClass\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 mclass\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-MClass\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 mwi\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-MWI\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 compress\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Compress\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 coding\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Coding\fs20\f0 . If unset, defaults to 0 (7 bits) if \fs18\f2 Content-Type\fs20\f0  is \fs18\f2 text/plain \fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 text/html\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 text/vnd.wap.wml\fs20\f0 . On \fs18\f2 application/octet-stream\fs20\f0 , defaults to 8 bits (1). All other \fs18\f2 Content-Type\fs20\f0  values are rejected.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 validity\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Validity\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 deferred\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Deferred\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-mask\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-DLR-Mask\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-DLR-Url\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 account\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Account\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 pid\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-PID\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-dcs\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Alt-DCS\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 binfo\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-BInfo\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 rpi\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-RPI\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 priority\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Priority\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx5160\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 meta-data\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Meta-Data\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart ID_kannelpost}{\*\bkmkend ID_kannelpost}\b\f1 Kannel POST\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Kannel can do POST if \fs18\f2 service\fs20\f0  is contains a \fs18\f2 post-url="..."\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _4684}{\*\bkmkend _4684}\b\lang1024 Table 7-12. X-Kannel Post Headers\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Parameter (escape code) equivalent\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 X-Kannel Header\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Notes\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %p (from)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-From\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Only sent if \fs18\f2 send-sender\fs20\f0  is true\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %P (to)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-To\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %t (time)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Time\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 %u (udh)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-UDH\