\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl234 \b\f1 Note: \b0 Don't forget to set POST Content-Type to \fs16\f2 text/xml\fs18\f1 !\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 There could be several \fs18\f2 da\fs20\f0  entries for \fs18\f2 sendsms-user \fs20\f0 to enable multi-recipient messages. \fs18\f2 da\fs20\f0  doesn't make sense in \fs18\f2 sms-service\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs18\f2 udh\fs20\f0  is the same format as \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-UDH\fs20\f0 . Example: <udh>06050415820000</udh>.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 On Kannel->application, \fs18\f2 from\fs20\f0  is the \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  that message arrives and \fs18\f2 to\fs20\f0  is the service name.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 On application->Kannel, \fs18\f2 from\fs20\f0  contains the credentials ( \fs18\f2 user/username\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 pass/password\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 account\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 to\fs20\f0  corresponds to the \fs18\f2 smsc-id \fs20\f0 to submit the message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs18\f2 user\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 username\fs20\f0  are equivalent and only one of them should be used. (same for \fs18\f2 pass\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 password\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 When application POST in Kannel, as in GET, only \fs18\f2 user\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 pass\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 da\fs20\f0  are required. Everything else is optional. (\fs18\f2 oa\fs20\f0  could be needed too is there's no \fs18\f2 default-sender\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 forced-sender\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp100\li1600\ri200\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp0\li1600\ri200\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\sl374\qc \b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Warning\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp100\li1600\ri200\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp0\li1600\ri200\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\sl234 \b0\fs18\lang1033 This is experimental code. XML format could and should change to fully met IETF's sms-xml standard (yet in draft) and additional tags needed by Kannel should be pondered.\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp100\li1600\ri200\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp0\li1600\ri200\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw40\brsp200\li1600\ri200\sl-100\keepn\par\pard\sl-1\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _4839}{\*\bkmkend _4839}\b\fs28\lang1024 SendSMS-user configurations\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To enable an SMS push, you must set \fs18\f2 sendsms-port\fs20\f0  into the 'smsbox' group and define one or more 'sendsms-user' groups. Each of these groups define one account, which can be used for the SMS push, via HTTP interface (see below)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _4843}{\*\bkmkend _4843}\b\lang1024 Table 7-13. SendSMS-User Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sendsms-user\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 username (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Name for the user/account. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 password (m)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password for the user (see HTTP interface, below) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 name\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As in 'sms-service' groups. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 user-deny-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 IP-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As other deny/allow IP lists, but for this user (i.e. this user is not allowed to do the SMS push HTTP request from other IPs than allowed ones). If not set, there is no limitations. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 user-allow-IP\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 IP-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 forced-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Force SMSC ID as a 'string' (linked to SMS routing, see 'smsc' groups) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 default-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If no SMSC ID is given with the send-sms request (see below), use this one. No idea to use with forced-smsc. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 default-sender\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 phone-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This number is set as sender if not set by \fs18\f2 from\fs20\f0  get/post parameter \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 faked-sender\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 phone-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As in 'sms-service' groups \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 max-messages\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 concatenation\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 split-chars\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 split-suffix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 omit-empty\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 bool\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 header\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 footer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of phone number prefixes which are accepted to be sent using this username. Multiple entries are separated with semicolon (';'). For example, "040;050" prevents sending of any SMS message with prefix of 040 or 050 through this SMSC. If denied-prefix is unset, only this numbers are allowed. If set, number are allowed if present in allowed or not in denied list. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 prefix-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A list of phone number prefixes which are NOT accepted to be sent using this username. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Load a list of accepted destinations of SMS messages. If a destination of an SMS message is not in this list, any message received from the HTTP interface is rejected. See notes of phone number format from numhash.h header file. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As white-list, but SMS messages from these numbers are automatically rejected. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  URL to be fetched if a \fs18\f2 dlr-mask\fs20\f0  CGI parameter is present. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A set of phone numbers which are accepted to be sent using this username. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  A set of phone numbers which may not send using this username. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines a set of accepted destinations of SMS messages. If a destination of an SMS message is not in this list, any message received from the HTTP interface is rejected. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  As \fs18\f2 white-list-regex\fs20\f0 , but SMS messages originating from a number matching the pattern are discarded. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Some sample 'sendsms-user' groups: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sendsms-user\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username = simple\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
password = elpmis\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sendsms-user\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username = complex\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
password = 76ftY\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
user-deny-ip = "*.*.*.*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
user-allow-ip = "123.234.123.234"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-messages = 3\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
concatenation = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
forced-smsc = SOL\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  The second one is very limited and only allows a user from IP "123.234.123.234". On the other hand, the user can send a longer message, up to 3 SMSes long, which is sent as concatenated SMS.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _4986}{\*\bkmkend _4986}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Over-The-Air configurations\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To enable Over-The-Air configuration of phones or other client devices that support the protocol you need to configure a \fs18\f2 sendsms-user\fs20\f0 .\fs18\f2 ota-setting\fs20\f0  group is not necessary, you can send settings to the phone as a XML document, but this method is perhaps more suitable for continuous provisioning.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you want to send multiple OTA configurations through the smsbox and you do not want to send XML documents, you will have to declare a \fs18\f2 ota-id\fs20\f0  string to the different \fs18\f2 ota-setting \fs20\f0 groups.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _4994}{\*\bkmkend _4994}\b\lang1024 Table 7-14. OTA Setting Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ota-setting\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ota-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An optional name or id for the ota-setting. Any string is acceptable, but semicolon ';' may cause problems, so avoid it and any other special non-alphabet characters. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 location\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The address of the HTTP server for your WAP services, i.e. \fs18\f2 http://wap.company.com\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 service\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Description of the service \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ipaddress\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 IP\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  IP address of your WAP gateway \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 phonenumber\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 phone-number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Phone number used to establish the PPP connection \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 speed\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Connection speed: 9600 or 14400. Defaults to 9600. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 bearer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Bearer type: data or sms. Defaults to data. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 calltype\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Call type: isdn or analog. Defaults to isdn. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 connection\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Connection type: cont or temp. Cont uses TCP port 9201 and Temp uses UDP port 9200. Defaults to cont. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 pppsecurity\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 on or off\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Enable CHAP authentication if set to on, PAP otherwise \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 authentication\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  normal or secure. Indicates whether WTLS should be used or not. Defaults to normal. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 login\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Login name. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 secret\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Login password \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 A sample 'ota-setting' group: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = ota-setting\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
location = http://wap.company.com\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
service = "Our company's WAP site"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ipaddress = 10.11.12.13\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
phonenumber = 013456789\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
bearer = data\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
calltype = analog\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
connection = cont\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
pppsecurity = off\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
authentication = normal\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
login = wapusr\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
secret = thepasswd\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  And a 'sendsms-user' to use with it. With concatenation enabled: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sendsms-user\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username = otauser\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
password = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-messages = 2\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
concatenation = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _5091}{\*\bkmkend _5091}\b\fs20\lang1024\f0 Table 7-15. OTA Bookmark Group Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ota-bookmark\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is a mandatory variable \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ota-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An optional name or id for the ota-bookmark. Any string is acceptable, but semicolon ';' may cause problems, so avoid it and any other special non-alphabet characters. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The address of the HTTP server for your WAP services, i.e. \fs18\f2 http://wap.company.com\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 name\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Description of the service \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 A sample 'ota-bookmark' group: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = ota-bookmark\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ota-id = wap-link\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
url = "http://wap.company.com"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
service = "Our company's WAP site"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  And a 'sendsms-user' to use with it, with the same conditions as for the 'ota-setting' group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _5127}{\*\bkmkend _5127}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Setting up more complex services\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The basic service system is very limited - it can only answer to original requester and it cannot send UDH data, for example. This chapter explains some more sophisticated and complex SMS service setups.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _5130}{\*\bkmkend _5130}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Redirected replies\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The basic service system always sends the answer back to original requester, but sometimes the content server needs to send something to other terminals or delay the answer. To create such systems, an SMS push is used.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The idea is to get the initial request, but then send no reply. Instead, the reply (if any) is sent via HTTP sendsms-interface as SMS Push. This way the service application has full control of the return content, and can do all needed formatting beforehand.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Note that when no reply is wanted, remember to set the variable \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  to zero (0) so that no reply is sent, unless an error occurs. Simple sample:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keyword = talk\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
get-url = "http://my.applet.machine/Servlet/talk?sender=%p&text=%r"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-messages = 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _5137}{\*\bkmkend _5137}\b\fs24\f1 Setting up operator specific services\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Those running Kannel with several SMS centers might need to define services according to the relying SMS center. To achieve this, first you need to give an ID name for SMS center connections (see above). Then use the \fs18\f2 accepted-smsc\fs20\f0  variable to define which messages can use that service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keyword = weather\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
accepted-smsc = SOL\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
get-url = "http://my.applet.machine/Servlet/weather?sender=%p&operator=SOL&text=%r"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _5142}{\*\bkmkend _5142}\b\fs24\f1 Setting up multi-operator Kannel\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Sometimes there is a need for Kannel to listen to two (or more) distinct SMS centers, and messages must be routed to services according to where they came from, and replies likewise must return to same SMSC. This is done via \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  magic. Here is a shortened sample configuration, which handles to distinct SMS servers and services:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-id = A\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
denied-smsc-id = B\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-id = B\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
denied-smsc-id = A\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
accepted-smsc = A\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
get-url = "..."\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
accepted-smsc = B\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
get-url = "..."\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 As can be seen, the \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  is used to identify the SMS center from which the message came. Then, the \fs18\f2 denied-smsc-id\fs20\f0  variable is used to prevent messages originally from the other SMS center from being sent through the other one. Finally 'sms-service' groups are defined with \fs18\f2 accepted-smsc\fs20\f0  so that they only accept messages from certain SMS center.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you want to use SMS push services, requesters should then set the \fs18\f2 smsc\fs20\f0  request parameter, or 'sendsms-user' groups should be defined like this:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sendsms-user\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username = operator_A\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
password = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
forced-smsc = A\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = sendsms-user\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username = operator_B\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
forced-smsc = B\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Note that if your SMS centers do not set the sender phone number but rely on number transmitted, you should set \fs18\f2 faked-sender\fs20\f0  to all 'sendsms-user' groups.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5156}{\*\bkmkend _5156}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Running SMS gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _5158}{\*\bkmkend _5158}\fs28 Using the HTTP interface to send SMS messages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 After you have configured Kannel to allow the sendsms service, you can send SMS messages via HTTP, e.g., using a WWW browser. The URL looks something like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http://smsbox.host.name:13013/cgi-bin/sendsms?\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username=foo&password=bar&to=0123456&text=Hello+world\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Thus, technically, you make an HTTP GET request. This means that all the information is stuffed into the URL. If you want to use this often via a browser, you probably want to make an HTML form for this.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Kannel will answer to sendsms request with following codes and body texts:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _5163}{\*\bkmkend _5163}\b\lang1024 Table 7-16. SMS Push reply codes\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Status\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Body\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Meaning\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 202\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 0: Accepted for delivery\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The message has been accepted and is delivered onward to a SMSC driver. Note that this status does not ensure that the intended recipient receives the message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 202\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 3: Queued for later delivery\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The bearerbox accepted and stored the message, but there was temporarily no SMSC driver to accept the message so it was queued. However, it should be delivered later on. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 4xx\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 (varies)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  There was something wrong in the request or Kannel was so configured that the message cannot be in any circumstances delivered. Check the request and Kannel configuration. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 503\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 Temporal failure, try again later.\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  There was temporal failure in Kannel. Try again later. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _5195}{\*\bkmkend _5195}\b\fs20 Table 7-17. SMS Push (send-sms) CGI Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 username\fs20\f0  (or \fs18\f2 user\fs20\f0 )\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Username or account name. Must be \fs18\f2 username\fs20\f0  of the one 'sendsms-user' group in the Kannel configuration, or results in 'Authorization failed' reply. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 password\fs20\f0  (or \fs18\f2 pass\fs20\f0 )\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password associated with given \fs18\f2 username\fs20\f0 . Must match corresponding field in the 'sendsms-user' group of the Kannel configuration, or 'Authorization failed' is returned. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 from\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Phone number of the sender. This field is usually overridden by the SMS Center, or it can be overridden by \fs18\f2 faked-sender\fs20\f0  variable in the \fs18\f2 sendsms-user\fs20\f0  group. If this variable is not set, smsbox \fs18\f2 global-sender\fs20\f0  is used. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 to\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 phone number list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Phone number of the receiver. To send to multiple receivers, separate each entry with \i space\i0  (' ', '+' url-encoded) - but note that this can be deactivated via \fs18\f2 sendsms-chars\fs20\f0  in the 'smsbox' group. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 text\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Contents of the message, URL encoded as necessary. The content can be more than 160 characters, but then \fs18\f2 sendsms-user\fs20\f0  group must have \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  set more than 1. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 charset\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Charset of text message. Used to convert to a format suitable for 7 bits or to UCS-2. Defaults to UTF-8 if coding is 7 bits and UTF-16BE if coding is UCS-2. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 udh\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional User Data Header (UDH) part of the message. Must be URL encoded. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional virtual smsc-id from which the message is supposed to have arrived. This is used for routing purposes, if any denied or preferred SMS centers are set up in SMS center configuration. This variable can be overridden with a \fs18\f2 forced-smsc\fs20\f0  configuration variable. Likewise, the \fs18\f2 default-smsc\fs20\f0  variable can be used to set the SMSC if it is not set otherwise. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 flash\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Deprecated. See \fs18\f2 mclass\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 mclass\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Sets the Message Class in DCS field. Accepts values between 0 and 3, for Message Class 0 to 3, A value of 0 sends the message directly to display, 1 sends to mobile, 2 to SIM and 3 to SIM toolkit. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 mwi\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Sets Message Waiting Indicator bits in DCS field. If given, the message will be encoded as a Message Waiting Indicator. The accepted values are 0,1,2 and 3 for activating the voice, fax, email and other indicator, or 4,5,6,7 for deactivating, respectively. This option excludes the \fs18\f2 flash\fs20\f0  option. {\*\bkmkstart ID_mwi_45_messages}{\*\bkmkend ID_mwi_45_messages}\up8\fs12 a\up0\fs20  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 compress\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Sets the Compression bit in DCS Field. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 coding\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Sets the coding scheme bits in DCS field. Accepts values 0 to 2, for 7bit, 8bit or UCS-2. If unset, defaults to 7 bits unless a udh is defined, which sets coding to 8bits. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 validity\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (minutes)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. If given, Kannel will inform SMS Center that it should only try to send the message for this many minutes. If the destination mobile is off other situation that it cannot receive the sms, the smsc discards the message. Note: you must have your Kannel box time synchronized with the SMS Center. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 deferred\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (minutes)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. If given, the SMS center will postpone the message to be delivered at now plus this many minutes. Note: you must have your Kannel box time synchronized with the SMS Center. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlrmask\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (bit mask)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Deprecated. See \fs18\f2 dlr-mask\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-mask\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number (bit mask)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Request for delivery reports with the state of the sent message. The value is a bit mask composed of: 1: Delivered to phone, 2: Non-Delivered to Phone, 4: Queued on SMSC, 8: Delivered to SMSC, 16: Non-Delivered to SMSC. Must set \fs18\f2 dlr-url\fs20\f0  on \fs18\f2 sendsms-user\fs20\f0  group or use the \fs18\f2 dlr-url\fs20\f0  CGI variable. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlrurl\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string (url)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Deprecated. See \fs18\f2 dlr-url\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string (url)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. If \fs18\f2 dlr-mask\fs20\f0  is given, this is the url to be fetched. (Must be url-encoded) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 pid\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 byte\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Sets the PID value. (See ETSI Documentation). Ex: SIM Toolkit messages would use something like \fs18\f2 &pid=127&coding=1&alt-dcs=1&mclass=3\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 alt-dcs\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. If unset, Kannel uses the alt-dcs defined on smsc configuration, or 0X per default. If equals to 1, uses FX. If equals to 0, force 0X. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 rpi\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Sets the Return Path Indicator (RPI) value. (See ETSI Documentation). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 account\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Account name or number to carry forward for billing purposes. This field is logged as ACT in the log file so it allows you to do some accounting on it if your front end uses the same username for all services but wants to distinguish them in the log. In the case of a HTTP SMSC type the account name is prepended with the service-name (username) and a colon (:) and forwarded to the next instance of Kannel. This allows hierarchical accounting. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 binfo\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Billing identifier/information proxy field used to pass arbitrary billing transaction IDs or information to the specific SMSC modules. For EMI2 this is encapsulated into the XSer 0c field, for SMPP this is encapsulated into the service_type of the submit_sm PDU. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 priority\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional. Sets the Priority value (range 0-3 is allowed). (Defaults to 0, which is the lowest priority). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li60\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Notes:\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\li380\ri100\sl260\fi-320 \tx320 a. \tab To set number of messages, use \fs18\f2 mwi=[0-3]&coding=0&udh=%04%01%02%<XX>%<YY>\fs20\f0 , where YY are the number of messages, in HEX, and XX are \fs18\f2 mwi\fs20\f0  plus 0xC0 if \fs18\f2 text\fs20\f0  field is not empty.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _5373}{\*\bkmkend _5373}\b\fs28\f1 Using the HTTP interface to send OTA configuration messages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 OTA messages can be sent to mobile phones or devices to auto-configure the settings for WAP. They are actually complex SMS messages with UDH and sent as concatenated messages if too long (and compiled if necessary).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You may either pass an HTTP request as GET method or POST method to the HTTP interface.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you want to send a configuration that is defined within Kannel's configuration file itself you have to pass a valid \fs18\f2 ota-id\fs20\f0  value otherwise the content of the request will be compiled to as OTA message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _5379}{\*\bkmkend _5379}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 OTA settings and bookmark documents\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Here is an example XML document (this one contains CSD settings for logging in to a mobile service; note that you must store DTD locally): \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<!DOCTYPE CHARACTERISTIC-LIST SYSTEM "file:///gw/settings.dtd">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<CHARACTERISTIC-LIST>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <CHARACTERISTIC TYPE="ADDRESS">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="BEARER" VALUE="GSM/CSD"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="PROXY" VALUE="10.11.12.13"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="PORT" VALUE="9201"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="CSD_DIALSTRING" VALUE="+12345678"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="PPP_AUTHTYPE" VALUE="PAP"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="PPP_AUTHNAME" VALUE="yourusername"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="PPP_AUTHSECRET" VALUE="yourauthsecret"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="CSD_CALLTYPE" VALUE="ISDN"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="CSD_CALLSPEED" VALUE="9600"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </CHARACTERISTIC>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <CHARACTERISTIC TYPE="URL" \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                  VALUE="http://wap.company.com/"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <CHARACTERISTIC TYPE="NAME">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="NAME" VALUE="Your WAP Company"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </CHARACTERISTIC>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</CHARACTERISTIC-LIST>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  A bookmark document looks like this: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<!DOCTYPE CHARACTERISTIC_LIST SYSTEM "file:///gw/settings.dtd">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<CHARACTERISTIC-LIST>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <CHARACTERISTIC TYPE="BOOKMARK">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="NAME" VALUE="WAP Company"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <PARM NAME="URL" VALUE="http://wap.company.com/"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </CHARACTERISTIC>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</CHARACTERISTIC-LIST>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Document type definition (DTD) for these documents is not available from Internet, you must supply it as a file. Kannel gw directory contains an example, \fs18\f2 settings.dtd\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _5385}{\*\bkmkend _5385}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 OTA syncsettings documents\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Used for the provisioning of sync configuration to SyncMl enabled handsets. Best supported by sonyericcsson terminals.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Sample syncsettings documents to set contacts, connection data and authentication: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<SyncSettings>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<Version>1.0</Version>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<HostAddr>http://syncml.server.com</HostAddr>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<RemoteDB>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <CTType>text/x-vcard</CTType>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <CTVer>2.1</CTVer>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <URI>contact</URI>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <Name>Address Book</Name>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</RemoteDB>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<Name>Synchonization</Name>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<Auth>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <AuthLevel>1</AuthLevel>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <AuthScheme>1</AuthScheme>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <Username>yourusername</Username>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <Cred>passwordbase64encoded</Cred>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</Auth>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</SyncSettings>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _5390}{\*\bkmkend _5390}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 OMA provisioning content\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 OMA provisioning allows the configuration of a wider range of settings than previously available in OTA terminals. Refer to OMA-WAP-ProvCont-v1_1-20021112-C (at http://www.openmobilealliance.org/tech/docs/) for details.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A shared secret (i.e. a pin or the phone IMSI) can be used to authenticate the settings. Defaults are 'userpin' and '1234' a. See the cgi variables 'sec' and 'pin' for available authentication options.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _5394}{\*\bkmkend _5394}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 GET method for the OTA HTTP interface\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 An example URL (OTA configuration defined in the Kannel configuration file): \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        http://smsbox.host.name:13013/cgi-bin/sendota?\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            otaid=myconfig&username=foo&password=bar&to=0123456\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  URL containing XML document looks like this (you must URL encode it before sending it over HTTP): \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        http://smsbox.host.name:13013/cgi-bin/sendota?\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            username=foo&password=bar&to=0123456&\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            text=MyURLEncodedXMLdocument&type=settings\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  You can send either settings, bookmark, syncsettings, or oma-settings, set CGI variable type accordingly. Default for this variable is settings.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _5401}{\*\bkmkend _5401}\b\lang1024 Table 7-18. OTA CGI Variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 otaid\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Name or ID of the 'ota-setting' group in Kannel configuration that should be sent to the phone. This variable is optional. If it is not given the first 'ota-setting' group is sent. This is unnecessary when a XML document is send to the phone. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 username\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Username of the 'sendsms-user' group in Kannel configuration, that has been configured to send OTA messages. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password associated with given \fs18\f2 username\fs20\f0 . Must match corresponding field in 'sendsms-user' group in Kannel configuration, or 'Authorization failed' is returned. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 to\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Number of the phone that is to receive the OTA configuration message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 from\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Phone number of the sender. This field is usually overridden by the SMS Center, or it can be overridden by faked-sender variable in the sendsms-user group. If this variable is not set, smsbox global-sender is used. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Optional virtual smsc-id from which the message is supposed to have arrived. This is used for routing purposes, if any denied or preferred SMS centers are set up in SMS center configuration. This variable can be overridden with a \fs18\f2 forced-smsc\fs20\f0  configuration variable. Likewise, the \fs18\f2 default-smsc\fs20\f0  variable can be used to set the SMSC if it is not set otherwise. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 text\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 XML document\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An URL encoded XML document, containing either settings or bookmarks. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 type\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Type of the XML document, supported values are "settings", "bookmarks", "syncsettings", and "oma-settings". Default is "settings". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 sec\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Security model used to authenticate oma-settings. One of: "userpin", "netwpin", "usernetwpin", or "userpinmac". \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 pin\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Authentication token. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 8. Setting up Push Proxy Gateway}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 8. Setting up Push Proxy Gateway}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_ppg}{\*\bkmkend ID_ppg}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 8. Setting up Push Proxy Gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains how to set up a push proxy gateway (PPG). An example configuration file are given. A working push proxy gateway is described. Routing wap pushes to a certain smsc and asking for \i SMS level\i0  delivery reports are described.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5472}{\*\bkmkend _5472}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Configuring ppg core group, for push initiator (PI) interface\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 PPG configuration group defines gateway's service interface. Configuring a PPG working with a trusted PI is easiest. Actually, you need no configuration at all: in this case a PPG with default values will be set up. Do not rely on this, default values may change. For PPG core configuration variables, see table 7.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 An example of a core configuration for PPG working only with specific addresses follows. Note that ppg-deny-ip-list is not actually necessary, but does make configuring simpler: IPs here are always denied, even when they are mentioned in the allowed IPs list.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Ppg-url is a simple stamp, used for routing requests to the right service. You can change this stamp by setting \fs18\f2 push-url\fs20\f0  configuration variable.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = ppg\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-url = /wappush\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-port = 8080\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
concurrent-pushes = 100\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
users = 1024\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-allow-ip = 194.100.32.125;127.0.0.1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-deny-ip = 194.100.32.89;194.100.32.103\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
trusted-pi = false\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _5479}{\*\bkmkend _5479}\b\fs20\f0 Table 8-1. PPG core group configuration variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 ppg\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value. Tells that we are configuring the PPG group. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ppg-port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The port PPG is listening at. Default 8080. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ppg-ssl-port (o)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value for PPG HTTPS support. The port at which PPG listens for HTTPS requests. There are no defaults; you must set the value separately. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-server-cert-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value for PPG HTTPS support. The file containing server's ssl certificate. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-server-key-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value for PPG HTTPS support. The file containing server's ssl private key. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ppg-url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  URL locating PPG services. Default \fs18\f2 /wappush \fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 global-sender\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Sender phone number required by some protocols. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 concurrent-pushes\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Number of concurrent pushes expected. Note that PPG \i does\i0  work even value is too low; it will only be slower. Default 100. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 users\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Number of actually configured user accounts. Note that PPG \i does\i0  work even value is too low; it will only be slower. Default 1024. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 trusted-pi\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If true, PI does authentication for PPG. Obviously, both of them must reside inside same firewall. Default true. If this variable is true, all security variables are ignored (even though they may be present). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ppg-deny-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 ip-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  PPG will not accept pushes from these IPs. Wild-cards are allowed. If this attribute is missing, no IP is denied \i by this list \i0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ppg-allow-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 ip-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  PPG will accept pushes from these, and only these, IPs. Wild-cards are allowed. Adding this list means that IPs not mentioned are denied, too. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 default-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If no SMSC ID is given with the wappush HTTP request (see below), use this one as default route for all push messages. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 default-dlr\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If no dlr url is given with the wappush HTTP request (see below), use this one as default route for all push messages. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ppg-smsbox-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  All ppg delivery reports are handled by this smsbox. This routes all DLR messages inside beaerbox to the specified smsbox for processing the HTTP requests to the DLR-URL. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 service-name\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is sms service name used by smsbox for wap push. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 default-dlr\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If no dlr url is given with the wappush HTTP request (see below), use this one as default route for all push messages. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 service-name\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  This is sms service name used by smsbox for wap push.. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 concatenation\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Segment wap push binary sms. Default on. You need not normally set this value. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 max-messages\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Maximum number of sm messages generated. Default 10. You need not set this variable, expect when your push documents are \i very\i0  long. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5613}{\*\bkmkend _5613}\b\fs34\f1 Configuring PPG user group variables\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 In addition of pi lists similar to the core group, ppg configuration specific to a certain user contains variables used for authentication and enforcing restrictions to phone numbers pi may contact. All variables are elaborated in table 7.2.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 As an example, let us see how to configure a ppg user (a pi, named here 'picom') allowed to send pushes only from a specified ip.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = wap-push-user\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
wap-push-user = picom\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
allow-ip = 62.254.217.163\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 It goes without saying that in real systems you must use more complex passwords than bar.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _5619}{\*\bkmkend _5619}\b\lang1024 Table 8-2. PPG user group configuration variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 wap-push-user\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value. Tells that we are configuring the users group. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 wap-push-user\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  (More) human readable name of an user. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ppg-username\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Username for this user. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ppg-password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Password for this user. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Phone number prefixes allowed in pushes coming from this pi. These prefixes must conform international phone number format. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-prefix\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Phone number prefixes denied in pushes coming from this pi. These prefixes must conform international phone number format. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines an url where from the white-list can be fetched. White list itself contains list of phone numbers accepting pushes from this pi. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines an url where from the blacklist can be fetched. Blacklist itself contains list of phone number not accepting pushes from this pi. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allow-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 ip-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines IPs where from this pi can do pushes. Adding this list means that IPs not mentioned are denied. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 deny-ip\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 ip-list\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines IPs where from this pi cannot do pushes. IPs not mentioned in either list are denied, too. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 default-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If no SMSC ID is given with the wappush HTTP request (see below), use this one as default route for this specific push user. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 forced-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Allow only routing to a defined SMSC ID for this specific push user. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 dlr-url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If no dlr is given with the wappush HTTP request (see below), use this one as default route for this specific push user. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsbox-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Smsbox handling delivery reports fro this user. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 forced-smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Allow only routing to a defined SMSC ID for this specific push user. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 white-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines the set of phone-numbers accepting pushes from this pi. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 black-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Defines the set of phone-numbers rejecting pushes from this pi. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 allowed-prefix-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set of phone number prefixes allowed in pushes coming from this pi. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 denied-list-regex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 POSIX regular expression\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set of phone number prefixes denied in pushes coming from this pi. See section on {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_regular_45_expressions}{\fldrslt Regular Expressions}} for details. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5742}{\*\bkmkend _5742}\b\fs34\f1 Finishing ppg configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 PPG uses SMS for sending SI to the phone and an IP bearer to fetch content specified by it (see chapter Overview of WAP Push). This means both wapbox and bearer smsc connections are in use. So your push proxy gateway configuration file must contain groups core, wapbox, smsc and smsbox. These are configured normal way, only smsc group may have push-specific variables. Note that following configurations are only an example, you may need more complex ones.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Bearerbox setup does not require any new variables:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = core\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-port = 13000\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsbox-port = 13001\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
wapbox-port = 13002\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-password = b\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
wdp-interface-name = "*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-file = "filename"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-level = 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
box-deny-ip = "*.*.*.*"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
box-allow-ip = "127.0.0.1"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
unified-prefix = "00358,0"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 You must set up wapbox, for pulling (fetching) the wap data, and of course starting the push itself. No new variables here, either.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = wapbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
bearerbox-host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-file = "filename"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-level = 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
syslog-level = none\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 To set up smsc connections, for pushing SI or SL over SMS. Here HTTP SMSC is used as an example. Variables no-sender and no-coding simplify HTTP request generated by Kannel. Send-url specifies content gateway, or sendsms service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = http\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-id = HTTP\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
port = 10000\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
system-type = kannel\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
no-sender = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
no-coding = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
send-url = http://host:port/path\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 To set up smsbox. This is used for ppg delivery reports, see later.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
bearerbox-host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsbox-id = dlrbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Kannel sources contain a sample push configuration file \fs18\f2 gw/pushkannel.conf\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5755}{\*\bkmkend _5755}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Running a push proxy gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Push proxy gateway is started by simply typing, using separate windows: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        gw/bearerbox [config-file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        gw/wapbox [config-file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  You can, of course, use more complex command line options.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5760}{\*\bkmkend _5760}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 An example using HTTP SMSC\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 An easy way to test and implement push services is to put ppg in the front of an existing sendsms service capable to send SMS data messages and to understand HTTP requests generated by HTTP SMSC. (See next chapter.) Then you need only configure SMSC configuration send-url to point to sendsms service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5763}{\*\bkmkend _5763}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 An example of minimum SI document\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Service indication (SI) should work with all types of phones, service loading does not. URL to be loaded is the main content of the document in both cases, however. Here an example (this is a minimum si document, not usable for testing, probably, but you want PPG to generate only one one SM per SI, if at all possible):\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<!DOCTYPE si PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD SI 1.0//EN"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
"http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/si.dtd">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<si>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <indication href="http://www.gni.ch"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        si-id="1@gni.ch">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            You have 4 new emails\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    </indication>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</si>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Note following points: a) Every SI must have different si-id. If there are none, href is used as an (very unsatisfactory) id. b) this si should not interrupt the phone's current action.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5768}{\*\bkmkend _5768}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 An example push (tokenized SI) document\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 HTTP SMSC generates a HTTP get request when it get a send-message event, expressed in Unicode. The content gateway, or the sendsms service must, of course, understand this URL. So here is an example, cgi variable text contains the url escaped form of a SI document. It is usable for testing prototype phones.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        http://matrix:8080/phplib/kannelgw.php?user=*deleted*&\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        pass=*deleted*=to=%2B358408676001&text=3D%02%06%17%AE%96localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        %3A8080%00%AF%80%8D%CF%B4%80%02%05j%00E%C6%0C%03wap.iobox.fi%00%11%03\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        1%40wiral.com%00%07%0A%C3%07%19%99%06%25%15%23%15%10%C3%04+%02%060%01\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        %03Want+to+test+a+fetch%3F%00%01%01&udh=%06%05%04%0B%84%23%F0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5773}{\*\bkmkend _5773}\fs34\f1 Default network and bearer used by push proxy gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If network and bearer attributes of the pap control document are missing or set any, Kannel uses address type for routing purposes: if the address type is a phone number (TYPE=PLMN), network defaults to GSM and bearer to SMS; if it is a IP-address (TYPE=IPv4), network defaults to GSM and bearer to CSD. So following minimal pap document works: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<!DOCTYPE pap PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD PAP//EN"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
          "http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/pap_1.0.dtd">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<pap>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <push-message push-id="9fjeo39jf084@pi.com">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <address address-value="WAPPUSH=+358408676001/TYPE=PLMN@ppg.carrier.com"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </push-message>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</pap>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5777}{\*\bkmkend _5777}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Push related Kannel headers\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter recapitulates Kannel headers used by ppg.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 PPG uses many Kannel headers. These are very similar as ones used by smsbox. (Both send sms to to the phone, after all.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _5781}{\*\bkmkend _5781}\b\lang1024 Table 8-3. Kannel headers used by PPG\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-SMSC\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Name of smsc used to deliver this push. Smsc configuration must contain some of corresponding variables, see \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-DLR-URL\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Url smsbox would call this url when doing the delivery report. Note that it can contain all Kannel escapes. See table 6.7 for details.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-DLR-Mask\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Mask telling smsbox when it should do the delivery reports. Values are same as used by smsbox, see chapter Delivery Reports for details.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Smsbox-Id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Tells which smsbox does the delivery report. Smsbox configuration must contain corresponding variable.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5814}{\*\bkmkend _5814}\b\fs34\f1 Requesting SMS level delivery reports for WAP pushes\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Push content is a normal binary SM, so you can ask delivery reports for them. These are useful for testing purposes (did the phone get the content at all, or did it just reject it.) Another use is create fall-back services for phones not supporting displaying a specific push content. (MMS one being perhaps currently most obvious.) Generally speaking, this service is very similar to smsbox one. See chapter Delivery Reports for details.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 For ppg sms delivery reports you will need a fully working smsbox. Add configuration variable \fs18\f2 smsbox-id\fs20\f0  to \fs18\f2 smsbox\fs20\f0  group (it is necessary, because there is no MT from any smsbox corresponding the delivery report bearerbox is receiving):\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       group = smsbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       smsbox-id = dlrbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       bearerbox-host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       log-file = "/var/log/kannel/smsbox-core.log"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       log-level = 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       access-log = "/var/log/kannel/smsbox-access.log"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Start smsbox normal way after updating the configuration file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You must add to PPG configuration file two less obvious variables: \fs18\f2 ppg-smsbox-id\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 service-name\fs20\f0 . \fs18\f2 Ppg-smsbox-id\fs20\f0  defines smsbox through which you want to route delivery reports, \fs18\f2 service-name\fs20\f0  makes possible for smsbox to handle wap pushes as a separate sms service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Setting \fs18\f2 ppg-smsbox-id\fs20\f0  will route all ppg messages through same smsbox. You can route delivery reports of separate user through separate smsboxes, by using configuration variable \fs18\f2 smsbox-id\fs20\f0  in group wap push user. Or you can use \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Smsbox-Id \fs20\f0 . This means routing every message separately.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You can supply dlr url and dlr mask as kannel header, or as a configuration variable in ppg user or ppg core group. (This is order of precedence, too.) First one is valid for only one message, second for a specific user, and third for every ppg user.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 So following is a minimum ppg core group for sms delivery reports:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = ppg\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-url = /wappush\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-port = 8080\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
concurrent-pushes = 100\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
trusted-pi = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
users = 1024\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
service-name = ppg\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-smsbox-id = dlrbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 And you can add Kannel headers to http post request. Here an example code snippet (C using Kannel gwlib; this example asks for all possible delivery reports).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http_header_add(push_headers, "X-Kannel-SMSC", "link0");\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http_header_add(push_headers, "X-Kannel-DLR-Url",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                "http://193.53.0.56:8001/notification-dlr?smsc-id=%i"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                "&status=%d&answer=%A&to=%P&from=%p&ts=%t");\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http_header_add(push_headers, "X-Kannel-DLR-Mask",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                octstr_get_cstr(dos = octstr_format("%d", 31)));\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http_header_add(push_headers, "X-Kannel-Smsbox-Id", "dlrbox"));\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Here status=%d tells the type of the delivery report and answer=%A the delivery report itself (sms content of it). Other ones are needed to map delivery report to original wap push.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 With these you can use with following http post request\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       http://193.53.0.56:8080/phplib/kannelgw.php?user=*deleted*&\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       pass=*deleted*=to=%2B358408676001&text=3D%02%06%17%AE%96localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       %3A8080%00%AF%80%8D%CF%B4%80%02%05j%00E%C6%0C%03wap.iobox.fi%00%11%03\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       1%40wiral.com%00%07%0A%C3%07%19%99%06%25%15%23%15%10%C3%04+%02%060%01\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       %03Want+to+test+a+fetch%3F%00%01%01&udh=%06%05%04%0B%84%23%F0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Note that you can use all sms service escapes in dlrurl, see Parameters (Escape Codes) for details.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you want to set dlr url for a specific user, you must set configuration variable \fs18\f2 dlr-url\fs20\f0 in \fs18\f2 wap-push-user\fs20\f0 , if for entire ppg, \fs18\f2 default-dlr-url \fs20\f0 in group \fs18\f2 ppg\fs20\f0 . Value must naturally match with one used in group smsc.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5846}{\*\bkmkend _5846}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Routing WAP pushes to a specific smsc\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains how to route wap push to a specific smsc.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Smsc routing for wap pushes is similar to sms pushes. So, firstly you must define configuration variable \fs18\f2 smsc-id \fs20\f0 in smsc group (or groups) in question. Say you used value \fs18\f2 link0\fs20\f0 . You can send this as a Kannel header:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
http_header_add(push_headers, "X-Kannel-SMSC", "link0");\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Then you can issue a request:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       http://193.53.0.56:8080/phplib/kannelgw.php?user=*deleted*&\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       pass=*deleted*=to=%2B358408676001&text=3D%02%06%17%AE%96localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       %3A8080%00%AF%80%8D%CF%B4%80%02%05j%00E%C6%0C%03wap.iobox.fi%00%11%03\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       1%40wiral.com%00%07%0A%C3%07%19%99%06%25%15%23%15%10%C3%04+%02%060%01\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       %03Want+to+test+a+fetch%3F%00%01%01&udh=%06%05%04%0B%84%23%F0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 You can use configuration variables to route all messages of a specific user or all ppg messages. Set \fs18\f2 default-smsc\fs20\f0  in group wap-push-user or in group ppg.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Again precedence of various methods of setting the smsc is kannel header, then configuration variable in group wap push user and then in group ppg.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 9. Using SSL for HTTP}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 9. Using SSL for HTTP}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_ssl_45_enabling}{\*\bkmkend ID_ssl_45_enabling}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 9. Using SSL for HTTP\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains how you can use SSL to ensure secure HTTP communication on both, client and server side.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Beware that the gateway, is acting in both roles of the HTTP model: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 1.\tab as HTTP client, i.e. for requesting URLs while acting as WAP gateway and while fetching information for the SMS services.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 2.\tab as HTTP server, i.e. for the administration HTTP interface, the PPG and for the sendsms HTTP interface.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260  That is why you can specify separate certification files within the core group to be used for the HTTP sides.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You can use one or both sides of the SSL support. There is no mandatory to use both if only one is desired.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5869}{\*\bkmkend _5869}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Using SSL client support\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To use the client support please use the following configuration directive within the core group\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = core\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ssl-client-certkey-file = "filename"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Now you are able to use https:// scheme URLs within your WML decks and SMS services.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5874}{\*\bkmkend _5874}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Using SSL server support for the administration HTTP interface\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To use the SSL-enabled HTTP server please use the following configuration directive within the core group\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = core\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
admin-port-ssl = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ssl-server-cert-file = "filename"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ssl-server-key-file = "filename"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5878}{\*\bkmkend _5878}\b\fs34\f1 Using SSL server support for the sendsms HTTP interface\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To use the SSL-enabled HTTP server please use the following configuration directive within the core and smsbox groups\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = core\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ssl-server-cert-file = "filename"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ssl-server-key-file = "filename"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
sendsms-port-ssl = true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5882}{\*\bkmkend _5882}\b\fs34\f1 Using SSL server support for PPG HTTPS interface\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If you want use PAP over HTTPS, (it is, a https scheme) add following directives to the ppg core group: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = ppg\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ppg-ssl-port = 8090\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ssl-server-cert-file = "/etc/kannel/cert1.pem"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
ssl-server-key-file = "/etc/kannel/key1.pem"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 PPG uses a separate port for HTTPS traffic, so so you must define it. This means that you can use both HTTP and HTTPS, when needed.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 10. SMS Delivery Reports}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 10. SMS Delivery Reports}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_delivery_45_reports}{\*\bkmkend ID_delivery_45_reports}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 10. SMS Delivery Reports\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains how to set up Kannel to deliver delivery reports.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Delivery reports are a method to tell your system if the message has arrived on the destination phone. There are different things which can happen to a message on the way to the phone which are:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Message gets rejected by the SMSC (unknown subscriber, invalid destination number etc).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Message gets accepted by the SMSC but the phone rejects the message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Message gets accepted by the SMSC but the phone is off or out of reach. The message gets buffered.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Message gets successfully delivered.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl234 \b\fs18\lang1024\f1 Note: \b0 If you want to use delivery reports, you must define a \fs16\f2 smsc-id\fs18\f1  for each smsc group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 When you deliver SMS to Kannel you have to indicate what kind of delivery report messages you would like to receive back from the system. The delivery report types currently implemented are: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 1: delivery success\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 2: delivery failure\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 4: message buffered\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 8: smsc submit\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 16: smsc reject\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260  If you want multiple report types, you simply add the values together. For example if you want to get delivery success and/or failure you set the \fs18\f2 dlr-mask\fs20\f0  value to 1+2. and so on. If you specify \fs18\f2 dlr-mask\fs20\f0  on the URL you pass on to Kannel you also need to specify \fs18\f2 dlr-url\fs20\f0 . \fs18\f2 dlr-url\fs20\f0  should contain the URL to which Kannel should place a HTTP requests once the delivery report is ready to be delivered back to your system.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 An example transaction would work as following. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 1. you send a message using dlr-mask=7 and dlr-url=http://www.xyz.com/cgi/dlr.php?myId=123456&type=%d\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 2. Kannel forwards the message to the SMSC and keeps track of the message\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 3. The SMSC can not reach the phone and thus returns a buffered message\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 4. Kannel calls http://www.xyz.com/cgi/dlr.php?myId=123456&type=4 to indicate the message being buffered\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 5. The phone is switched on and the SMS gets delivered from the SMSC. The SMSC reports this to Kannel\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 6. Kannel calls http://www.xyz.com/cgi/dlr.php?myId=123456&type=1 to indicate the final success\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl234 \b\fs18\f1 Note: \b0 If you put your own message ID in the dlr-url like in the example above, you can then use this ID to update your database with the message status.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Depending on the SMSC type not all type of messages are supported. For example a CIMD SMSC does not support buffered messages. Also some SMSC drivers have not implemented all DLR types.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 11. Getting help and reporting bugs}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 11. Getting help and reporting bugs}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_bug_45_reporting}{\*\bkmkend ID_bug_45_reporting}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 11. Getting help and reporting bugs\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter explains where to find help with problems related to the gateway, and the preferred procedure for reporting bugs and sending corrections to them.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The Kannel development mailing list is devel@kannel.org. To subscribe, send mail to devel-subscribe@kannel.org (mailto:devel-subscribe@kannel.org). This is currently the best location for asking help and reporting bugs. Please include configuration file and version number.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix A. Upgrading notes}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix A. Upgrading notes}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_upgrading_45_notes}{\*\bkmkend ID_upgrading_45_notes}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix A. Upgrading notes\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This appendix includes pertinent information about required changes on upgrades.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5944}{\*\bkmkend _5944}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 From 1.2.x or 1.3.1 to 1.3.2 and later\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16\lang1033\f0 \'95\tab \fs20 1. \fs18\f2 at\fs20\f0  module was dropped and \fs18\f2 at2\fs20\f0  module is now called \fs18\f2 at\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\f0 \'95\tab \fs20 2. \fs18\f2 emi\fs20\f0  module was renamed to \fs18\f2 emi_x25\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 emi_ip\fs20\f0  sub-module was dropped and \fs18\f2 emi2\fs20\f0  is now called \fs18\f2 emi\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix B. Certificate generation}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix B. Certificate generation}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_certificates}{\*\bkmkend ID_certificates}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix B. Certificate generation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This appendix includes pertinent information about required SSL certificate genaration, where needed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _5963}{\*\bkmkend _5963}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Self-signed 1024-bit RSA SSL certificates using openssl\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 1.\tab Generate private key:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs18\f2 openssl genrsa -des3 -out server.key 1024\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs20\f0 You will be asked for a passphrase.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 2.\tab Generate a certificate request:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs18\f2  openssl req -new -key server.key -out server.csr \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs20\f0 Several questions follow. At the end you may send server.csr to a certificate authority, which in turn will sign it and generate the certificate for you, or you can sign it yourself.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 3.\tab Remove passphrase from key:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs18\f2 cp server.key server.key.org\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 openssl rsa -in server.key.org -out server.key \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 rm server.key.org\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \fs20\f0 4.\tab Self-sign the certificate:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 If you chose not to send the request to a Certificate Authority, you will need to sign it yourself. This one is good for 1 year:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs18\f2 openssl x509 -req -days 365 -in server.csr -signkey server.key -out server.crt\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \fs20\f0 5.\tab Move keys to desired location:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs18\f2 mv server.crt /etc/kannel/public/server.crt\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 mv server.key /etc/kannel/private/server.key \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 mv server.csr /etc/ianwap/private/ianwap.csr (key request)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Update configuration accordingly\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix C. Using the fake WAP sender}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix C. Using the fake WAP sender}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _5999}{\*\bkmkend _5999}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix C. Using the fake WAP sender\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This appendix explains how to use the fake WAP sender to test the gateway.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix D. Using the fake SMS center}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix D. Using the fake SMS center}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _6002}{\*\bkmkend _6002}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix D. Using the fake SMS center\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Fakesmsc is a simple testing tool to test out Kannel and its SMS services. It \i cannot\i0  be used to send messages to mobile terminals, it is just a simulated SMS center with no connection to real terminals.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6006}{\*\bkmkend _6006}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Setting up fakesmsc\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This section sums up needed steps to set up system for fakesmsc use.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6009}{\*\bkmkend _6009}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Compiling fakesmsc\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The fake SMS center should compile at the same time as main Kannel compiles. The outcome binary, \fs18\f2 fakesmsc\fs20\f0 , is in \fs18\f2 test\fs20\f0  directory. The source code is quite simple and trivial, and is easily edited.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6014}{\*\bkmkend _6014}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Configuring Kannel\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To use \fs18\f2 fakesmsc\fs20\f0  to test out Kannel, you have to add it to main configuration file (see above). The simplest form for this configuration group is like this:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = fake\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
port = 10000\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 The fakesmsc configuration group accepts all common 'smsc' configuration group variables, like \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 preferred-smsc-id\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 denied-smsc-id\fs20\f0 , which can be used to test out routing systems and diverted services, before setting up real SMS center connections. If you include a fakesmsc group when bearerbox is connected to real SMS centers, you should add the \fs18\f2 connect-allow-ip\fs20\f0  variable to prevent unauthorized use.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To set up multiple fakesmsc'es, just add new groups. Remember to put a different port number to each one.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6025}{\*\bkmkend _6025}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Running Kannel with fakesmsc connections\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 After configuring Kannel, you can start testing it. The bearerbox will listen for fakesmsc client connections to the port(s) specified in the configuration file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6028}{\*\bkmkend _6028}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Starting fake SMS center\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Each fakesmsc is started from command line, with all sent messages after command name. If any options are used (see below), they are put between the command and the messages. The usage is as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
test/fakesmsc [options] <message1> [message2 ...]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Options and messages are explained below, but as a quick example, a typical startup can go like this:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
test/fakesmsc -i 0.1 -m 100 "100 200 text nop" "100 300 text echo this"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 This tells fakesmsc to connect to bearerbox at localhost:10000 (default) and send a hundred messages with an interval of 0.1 seconds. Each message is from number 100, and is either to number 200 with message 'nop' or to 300 with message 'echo this'.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Messages received from bearerbox are shown in the same format (described below).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _6036}{\*\bkmkend _6036}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Fake messages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Each message consists of four or five parts: sender number, receiver number, type, udh (if present) and main message itself. Sender and receiver numbers do not mean anything except for log files and number-based routing in Kannel.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The parts of a message are separated with spaces. As each message is taken as one argument, it must be put in quotation marks.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Message type must be one of the following: "text", "data", "ucs2", "udh-data", "udh-text", "route" and "dlr-mask". Here are some examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
test/fakesmsc -i 0.01 -v 1 -m 1000 "100 300 text echo this message"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
test/fakesmsc -i 0.01 -m 1000 "100 300 data echo+these+chars%03%04%7f"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
test/fakesmsc -m 1 "100 500 udh-data %0eudh+stuff+here main+message"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 For "text", the rest of the argument is taken as the literal message. For "data", the next part must be the url-encoded version of the message. Space is coded as '+'. For "ucs2", the next part must be the url-encoded version of the message. For "udh-data", the next 2 parts are the UDH and main message. Both must be in url-encoded form. For "udh-text", the UDH part must be url-encoded, and the message is taken as literals. For "route" both values are literal, where the first value defines the smsbox route destination. For "dlr-mask" noth values are literal, where the first value defines the numerical DLR event.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If multiple messages are given, fakesmsc randomly chooses one for each sending.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _6044}{\*\bkmkend _6044}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Interactive mode\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If no messages are passed on the command line when starting fakesmsc, it runs on "interactive mode".\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 When running on this mode, fakesmsc accept single messages typed on stdin (without quotation marks) and displays the responses on stdout. This allows for a more finely controlled interaction with the backend applications.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _6048}{\*\bkmkend _6048}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Fakesmsc command line options\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Fake SMS center can be started with various optional command line arguments.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _6051}{\*\bkmkend _6051}\b\lang1024 Table D-1. Fakesmsc command line options\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Switch\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -H\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 host\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use host \i host\i0  instead of default \fs18\f2 localhost\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -r\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 port\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use port number \i port\i0  instead of default 10000. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -i\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 interval\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use message interval \i interval\i0  (in seconds, fractions accepted) instead of default interval 1.0 seconds. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -m\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 max\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Send a maximum of \i max\i0  messages. Value -1 means that an unlimited number of messages is sent. Default -1. Using 0 can be useful to listen for messages sent via other channels. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 In addition, fakesmsc accepts all common Kannel {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_arguments_46_title}{\fldrslt \i Command line options}} like \fs18\f2 --verbosity\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix E. Setting up a test environment for Push Proxy Gateway}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix E. Setting up a test environment for Push Proxy Gateway}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _6092}{\*\bkmkend _6092}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix E. Setting up a test environment for Push Proxy Gateway\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This appendix explains how to set a test environment for PPG. This contains a simulated SMSC, for instance a http server simulation (this is used as example, because it is simplest) and a simulated push initiator. Between them, there is the push proxy gateway to be tested. This means that you must configure HTTP SMSC.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6095}{\*\bkmkend _6095}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Creating push content and control document for testing\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Here is an example of a push control document, which gives PPG instructions how to do the pushing. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
