      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6110}{\*\bkmkend _6110}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Starting necessary programs\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 PPG test environment contains, in addition of wapbox and bearerbox, two test programs, \fs18\f2 test_ppg\fs20\f0  (simulating push initiator) and \fs18\f2 test_http_server\fs20\f0  (simulating a SMSC center accepting pushed content send over SMS. You can find both of these programs in \fs18\f2 test\fs20\f0  directory, and they both are short and easily editable.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To set up a test environment, you must first configure a push proxy gateway (setting flag trusted-pi true makes testing easier). This explained in Chapter "Setting up push proxy gateway". Then issue following commands, in Kannel's root directory and in separate windows: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        gw/bearerbox [config-file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        gw/wapbox [config-file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  Of course you can use more complicated wapbox and bearerbox command line options, if necessary.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To run a http smsc, start http server simulation: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        test/test_http_server -p port\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  You can, of course, select the port at will. Remember, though, that PPG listens at the port defined in the ppg configuration file. Other \fs18\f2 test_http_server\fs20\f0  options are irrelevant here.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Lastly, start making push requests, for instance with a test program \fs18\f2 test_ppg\fs20\f0 . Its first argument is a URL specifying location of push services. Other arguments are two file names, first one push content and second one pap control document. (For command line options, see Table C.1.). For example doing one push(you can simplify push url by setting a ppg configuration variable, see "Setting up push proxy gateway"; q flag here prevents dumping of test_ppg program debugging information):\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        test/test_ppg -q http://ppg-host-name:ppg-port/ppg-url [content_file] \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        [control_file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This presumes that you have set trusted-pi true.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you want use authentication in a test environment, you can pass username and password either using headers (setting flag -b) or url (you must have set trusted-pi false and added wap-push-user configuration group):\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        test/test_ppg -q http://ppg-host-name:ppg-port?username=ppg-username'&'\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        password=ppg-password [content_file] [control_file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _6131}{\*\bkmkend _6131}\fs20\f0 Table E-1. Test_ppg's command line options\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Switch\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -c\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use content qualifier \i string\i0  instead of default \fs18\f2 si\fs20\f0  (service indication). Allowed values are wml, si, sl, sia, multipart, nil and scrap. Nil and scrap are used for debugging purposes. Wml does work with some older phone simulators. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -a\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use application id \i string\i0  instead of default \fs18\f2 any\fs20\f0 . Application identifies the application in the phone that should handle the push request. Sia, ua, mms, nil and scrap are accepted. Nil and scrap are used for debugging purposes. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -e\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use transfer encoding when sending a push content. Only base64 is currently supported. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -b\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use headers for authentication, instead of url. Default off. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -i\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Wait interval \i number\i0  instead of default \fs18\f2 0\fs20\f0  between pushes. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -r\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Do \i number\i0  requests instead of default \fs18\f2 1\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -t\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use \i number\i0  threads instead of default \fs18\f2 1\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -s\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use \fs18\f2 string\fs20\f0  as a X-WAP-Application-Id header (You must supply whole header). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -I\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Use \fs18\f2 string\fs20\f0  as a X-WAP-Initiator-URI header (You must supply whole header). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -B\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, accept binary content. Default is off. \fs18\f2 1\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -d\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 enumerated string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Set delimiter to be used. Acceptable values are crlf and lf. Default is \fs18\f2 crlf\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -p\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  If set, add an preamble (hard-coded one). Default is off. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 -E\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  if set, add an epilogue (hard-coded). Default is off. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6232}{\*\bkmkend _6232}\b\fs34\f1 Using Nokia Toolkit as a part of a developing environment\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This chapter describes a developing environment using Nokia Toolkit instead of \fs18\f2 test_http_server\fs20\f0  program.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You cannot use a real phone for testing a push server. Sending random messages to a phone does not work, because its only feedback (if it works properly) in error situations is dropping the offending message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Nokia Toolkit, instead, displays push headers, decompiles tokenized documents and outputs debugging information. It is not, of course, a carbon copy of a real phone. But it is still useful for checking spec conformance of push servers.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Toolkit runs on Windows, the first thing you must is to install a virtual machine (VMWare is one possibility) in the machine where Kannel runs. Then you must configure Toolkit for working with a push gateway.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Then start \fs18\f2 bearerbox\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 wapbox \fs20\f0 similar way as told before. You must set the correct client address in the push document send by \fs18\f2 test_ppg\fs20\f0  program. Use IP address of our virtual machine (easiest way to get this is to ping your virtual machine name in the dos prompt window). Your bearer is in this case IP. An example pap document follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<!DOCTYPE pap PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD PAP//EN"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
          "http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/pap_1.0.dtd">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<pap>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <push-message push-id="9fjeo39jf084@pi.com"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                deliver-before-timestamp="2001-09-28T06:45:00Z"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                deliver-after-timestamp="2001-02-28T06:45:00Z"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                progress-notes-requested="false">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <address address-value="WAPPUSH=192.168.214.1/TYPE=IPV4@ppg.carrier.com"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <quality-of-service priority="low"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                        delivery-method="unconfirmed"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    </quality-of-service>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </push-message>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</pap>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Note address-value format. It is contains type and value, because PAP protocol supports different address formats.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You must use \fs18\f2 test_ppg\fs20\f0 's -a and -c flags when pushing messages to Toolkit. -A defines the client application handling pushes, right value for it is ua. -C defines the content type of your push message. SI works with all Toolkits, wml only with some older versions.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6247}{\*\bkmkend _6247}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Testing PAP protocol over HTTPS\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 When testing HTTPS connection to PPG, you probably want use test_ppg's configuration file, because number of required parameters is quite high. Here is a example test_ppg configuration file:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 group = test-ppg\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 retries = 2\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 pi-ssl = yes\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 ssl-client-certkey-file = /etc/kannel/certkey.pem\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 group = configuration\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 push-url = https://localhost:8900/wappush\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 pap-file = /etc/kannel/ipnoqos.txt\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 content-file = /etc/kannel/si.txt\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 username = foo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 password = bar\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 With a configuration file, you can do a push by typing:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        test/test_ppg -q [configuration_file]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _6254}{\*\bkmkend _6254}\fs20\f0 Table E-2. Test_ppg's configuration file directives\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Directive\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 test_ppg\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory parameter. Start of test_ppg's core group. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 retries\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 number\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  The client tries to log in to PPG \i number\i0  times before discarding the push request. Default is 2. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 pi-ssl\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 boolean\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory parameter for HTTPS connection. Does the client use HTTPS connection. Default is no. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-client-certkey-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory parameter for HTTPS connection. File containing the client's ssl certificate and private key. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 ssl-trusted-ca-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory parameter for HTTPS connection.This file contains the certificates test_ppg is willing to trust. If this directive is not set, certificates are not validated and HTTPS would not be tested. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 group\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 configuration\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory parameter. Start of test_ppg's test group. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 push-url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 url\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value. URL locating PPG's services. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 pap-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value. File containing pap request's control document. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 content-file\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 filename\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value. File containing pap request's content document. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 username\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value. PPG service user's username. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 password\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \i\fs20 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Mandatory value. PPG service user's password. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix F. Setting up a dial-up line}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix F. Setting up a dial-up line}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _6329}{\*\bkmkend _6329}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix F. Setting up a dial-up line\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This appendix explains how to set up a dial-up line in Linux for use with the Kannel WAP gateway. In order for it to work you need a Linux kernel with PPP capabilities. Most distributions provides PPP kernel support by default. For more information how to compile PPP support into the kernel please read the "Linux Kernel HOWTO" at http://www.linuxdoc.org/.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6332}{\*\bkmkend _6332}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Analog modem\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This section explains how to set up a dial-up line with an analog modem.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Download and install the mgetty package.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        rpm -ivh mgetty-VERSION-rpm\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To run mgetty as a daemon, add the following line to /etc/inittab.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Read man inittab for more detailed information. In this example we assume your modem is connected to the serial port ttyS0 (COM 1).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        S0:2345:respawn:/sbin/mgetty ttyS0 -x 6 -D /dev/ttyS0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 We need to start the pppd automatically when mgetty receives an AutoPPP request. Add the next line to /etc/mgetty+sendfax/login.config\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        /AutoPPP/ - - /usr/sbin/pppd file /etc/ppp/options.server\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 In /etc/mgetty+sendfax/mgetty.config you might need to change the connect speed between the computer and the modem. Note: this is not the connect speed between the WAP client and the server modem. If you are e.g. going to use a Nokia 7110 as the server side modem you need to change the speed to 19200. Usually you can just leave the speed to the default value (38400).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        speed 38400\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Add the following lines to /etc/ppp/options.server\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        refuse-chap\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        require-pap\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        lock\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        modem\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        crtscts\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        passive\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        192.168.1.10:192.168.1.20\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        debug\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 In /etc/ppp/pap-secrets add the username and password for the ppp account. The IP address is the one assigned to the phone.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        wapuser * wappswd 192.168.0.20\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Configure your phone (this example is for Nokia 7110)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        homepage http:/yourhost/hello.wml\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        connection type continuous\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        connection security off\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        bearer data\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        dial up number (your phone number)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        ip address (IP of host running bearerbox)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        auth type normal\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        data call type analogue\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        data call speed 9600\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        username wapuser\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        password wappswd\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6357}{\*\bkmkend _6357}\fs34\f1 ISDN terminal\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This section needs to be written\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix G. Regular Expressions}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix G. Regular Expressions}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_regular_45_expressions}{\*\bkmkend ID_regular_45_expressions}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix G. Regular Expressions\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Note: this is not intended to be an introduction to regular expressions (short: regex) but a description of their application within Kannel. For general information regarding regexes please refer to {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_Biblio_45_regexp}{\fldrslt [Biblio-regexp]}}.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6364}{\*\bkmkend _6364}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Syntax and semantics of the regex configuration parameter\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This section describes the regex-configuration parameters and their effects in combination with the respective non-regex-parameter, e.g. \fs18\f2 white-list\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 white-list-regex\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6369}{\*\bkmkend _6369}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 How-to setup the regex-parameters\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 examples, short syntax, what happens on errors Regex-parameters are configured just as every other parameter is configured. Regular expressions are supported as defined by POSIX Extended Regular Expressions. Suppose a configuration where only SMS messages originating from a sender using a number with a prefix of "040", "050", "070" or "090" are accepted. Without regexes the configuration would read \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                allowed-prefix="040;050;070;090"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  Using regular expressions yields a more concise configuration \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                allowed-prefix-regex=^0[4579]0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  The following table gives an overview over some regex-operators and their meaning, the POSIX Regular Expressions manual page (regex(7)). Once again, the extended regex-syntax is used and the table is just meant as a means to give a quick-start to regular expressions, the next section features some more complex examples. \sa200\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\lang1033\f1 Operator\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\lang1033\f1 Meaning\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 |\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 or, for example "dog|hog" matches dog or hog.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 \{number,number\}\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 repetition, for example "a\{2,5\}" matches - among others - "aa", "aaa" and "baaaaad"\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 *\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 shorthand for \{0,\}\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 ?\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 shorthand for \{0,1\}\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 +\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 shorthand for \{1,\}\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 []\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 bracket expression, defines a class of possible single character matches. For example "[hb]og" matches "hog" and "bog". If the expression starts with \fs18\f2 ^\fs20\f0  then the class is negated, e.g. "[^hb]og" does not match "hog" and "bog" but matches for example "dog".\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 ()\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 groups patterns, e.g. "[hb]o(g|ld)" matches "hog", "hold", "bog", "bold" \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 [:class:]\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 A character class such as digit, space etc. See wctype(3) for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 ^\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Start of line anchor.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 $\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 End of line anchor.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033  The advantages of regular expressions are at hand \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Regexes are easier to understand, if one is fluent in POSIX Regular Expressions. Although simple expressions as shown above should be clear to everyone who has ever used a standard UN*X shell.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Regexes are easier to maintain. Suppose the example above needed to cope with dozens of different prefixes each with subtle differences, in such cases using a - carefully constructed - regular expression could help to keep things in apple pie order. Furthermore regexes help reducing redundancy within the configuration.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Regexes more flexible than standard parameters.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260  Nevertheless, it must be mentioned that - in addition to the overhead involved - complexity is an issue, too. Although the syntactic correctness of each used regular expression is ensured (see below) the semantic correctness cannot be automatically proofed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Expressions that are not compilable, which means they are not valid POSIX regexes, force Kannel to panic with a message like (note the missing "]") \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ERROR: gwlib/regex.c:106: gw_regex_comp_real: regex compilation `[hbo(g|ld)' failed: Invalid regular expression (Called from gw/urltrans.c:987:create_onetrans.)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                PANIC: Could not compile pattern '[hbo(g|ld)'\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  As shown the erroneous pattern is reported in the error message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6432}{\*\bkmkend _6432}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Regex and non-regex-parameters\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Using the regex and non-regex version of a parameter at the same time should be done with caution. Both are combined in a boolean-or sense, for example \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                white-list=01234\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                white-list-regex=^5(23)?$\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0  implies that a number is accepted either if it is "01234", "5" or "523" - note the use of anchors! The same goes for all the other parameters, thus both mechanisms can be used without problems in parallel, but care should be taken that the implications are understood and wanted.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6436}{\*\bkmkend _6436}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Performance issues\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 While there is some overhead involved, the actual performance degradation is negligible. At startup - e.g. when the configuration files are parsed - the regular expressions are pre-compiled and stored in the pre-compiled fashion, thus future comparisons involve executing the expression on some string only. To be on the sure side, before using regexes extensively some benchmarking should be performed, to verify that the loss of performance is acceptable.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6439}{\*\bkmkend _6439}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Examples\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This section discusses some simple scenarios and potential solutions based on regexes. The examples are not meant to be comprehensive but rather informative.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6442}{\*\bkmkend _6442}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 Example 1: core-configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The bearerbox must only accept SMS messages from three costumers. The first costumer uses numbers that always start with "0824" the second one uses numbers that start with either "0123" and end in "314" or start with "0882" and end in "666". The third costumer uses numbers starting with "0167" and ending in a number between "30" and "57".\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Important in this and in the following examples is the use of anchors, otherwise a "string contains" semantic instead of a "string is equal" semantic would be used.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                group=core\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                white-list-regex=^((0824[0-9]+)|(0123[0-9]+314)|(0882[0-9]+666)|(0167[0-9]+([34][0-9]|5[0-7])))$\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6447}{\*\bkmkend _6447}\b\fs28\f1 Example 3: smsc-configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Only SMS messages originating from certain SMSCs (\fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  is either "foo", "bar" or "blah") are preferably forwarded to this smsc. Furthermore all SMSCs with an id containing "vodafone" must never be forwarded to the smsc. Not the missing anchors around "vodafone".\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                group=smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                preferred-smsc-id-regex=^(foo|bar|blah)$\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                denied-prefix-regex=vodafone\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6452}{\*\bkmkend _6452}\b\fs28\f1 Example 4: sms-service-configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Please note that there are a mandatory \fs18\f2 keyword\fs20\f0  and an optional \fs18\f2 keyword-regex\fs20\f0  fields. That means that service selection can be simplified as in the following example. Suppose that some Web-content should be delivered to the mobile. Different costumers use the same service but they rely on different keywords. Whenever a sms-service is requested, Kannel first checks whether a regex has been defined, if not a literal match based on \fs18\f2 keyword\fs20\f0  is performed. If a regex is configured then the literal match is never tried.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                group=sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                keyword=web_service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                keyword-regex=^(data|www|text|net)$\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                get-url=http://someserver.net/getContent.jsp\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix H. Meta Data}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix H. Meta Data}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart ID_meta_45_data}{\*\bkmkend ID_meta_45_data}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix H. Meta Data\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This appendix describes the elements and syntax required to access meta-data coming from and going to SMSC connections.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6462}{\*\bkmkend _6462}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Overview\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Many SMSC services have means to exchange data that doesn't conform to the established format\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 For example, the SMPP protocol defines the TLV (Tag, Length, Value) mechanism to allow adding arbitrary parameters to it's PDU's, thus extending it's functionality.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To achieve this, a special group and parameters were added to abstract the meta-data into something that could be used disregarding the SMSC type.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Currently only the SMPP module is implemented, but the format chosen allows for other modules to be added in the future.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6468}{\*\bkmkend _6468}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 SMPP Implementation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 A special group type \fs18\f2 smpp-tlv\fs20\f0  is needed for each TLV. Each group defines the address, data type and length of the TLV\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Example configuration:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smpp-tlv\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
name = my_tlv_name\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
tag = 0x1601\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
type = octetstring\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
length = 20\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc-id = a;b;c\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Data Type\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 tag\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 hex\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  32-Bit HEX address for the given TLV. Must match SMPP specifications for the TLV (or be agreed with the SMSC operator). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 type\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Accepted data type. Accepted values are \fs18\f2 integer\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 nulterminated\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 octetstring\fs20\f0 . Must match SMPP specifications for the TLV (or be agreed with the SMSC operator). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 length\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 integer\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Maximum data length expected in bytes. Must match SMPP specifications for the TLV (or be agreed with the SMSC operator). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 smsc-id\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An optional smsc-id for which this TLV is valid. If smsc-id is not given then this TLV is valid for all SMSCs. To define list of smsc-id, just use ; as split char. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 const\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 string\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  An optional constant value which is used if no value is set for this TLV via meta-data. \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Beside defined in the configuration \fs18\f2 smpp-tlv\fs20\f0  group, SMPP also supports some hardcoded \fs18\f2 meta-data\fs20\f0  values: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 data_coding\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 source_addr_ton\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 source_addr_npi\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 dest_addr_ton\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 dest_addr_npi\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6529}{\*\bkmkend _6529}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 MO Messages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Meta Data coming from MO messages comes urlencoded into the \fs18\f2 %D\fs20\f0  parameter. Only TLV parameters defined on the configuration file are honored.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _6533}{\*\bkmkend _6533}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Example\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs18\lang1033\f2 http://localhost/myscript?...&meta-data=%D&...\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _6537}{\*\bkmkend _6537}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 MT Messages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To send into an MT messages the \fs18\f2 meta-data\fs20\f0  parameter should be used.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The format used to pass the data has 2 parts: The \fs18\f2 ?smsc-type?\fs20\f0  (surrounded by question marks), which specify the kind of smsc receiving the data (at the moment only \fs18\f2 smpp\fs20\f0  is implemented) and then a set of key/value pairs with the data to be transmitted. Extra smsc-types can be added surrounded by question marks and followed by the key/value pairs.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In other words, the data should be coded using the following format, where URLENCODE() implies that the data between parentheses should be urlencoded:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs18\f2 ?URLENCODE(<smsc-type1>)?URLENCODE(key1)=URLENCODE(value1)&URLENCODE(key2)=URLENCODE(value2)&...\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 ?URLENCODE(<smsc-type2>)?URLENCODE(key1)=URLENCODE(value1)&...\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Only the parameters defined on the configuration file are honored.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Important: For the sendsms interface, you must URLENCODE the resulting string again to pass it as a single parameter on the URL.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _6551}{\*\bkmkend _6551}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Examples\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If we want to send the parameter "my-data" with value "Hello World" to an SMSC over SMPP, we'd use:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 sendsms get-url:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs18\f2 http://localhost:13013/cgi-bin/sendsms?...&meta-data=%3Fsmpp%3Fmy-data%3DHello%2BWorld\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 sendsms post:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Send \fs18\f2 ?smpp?my-data=Hello+World\fs20\f0  on the \fs18\f2 X-Kannel-Meta-Data\fs20\f0  header\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 XML:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs18\f2 <meta-data>?smpp?my-data=Hello+World</meta-data>\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix I. Log files}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Appendix I. Log files}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _6564}{\*\bkmkend _6564}\b\fs41\f1 Appendix I. Log files\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This appendix describes the log file format.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6567}{\*\bkmkend _6567}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Bearerbox Access Log\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b0\fs16\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        2001-01-01 12:00:00 Sent SMS [SMSC:smsc] [SVC:sms] [ACT:account] [BINFO:binfo] [FID:1234567890] [META:meta]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            [from:12345] [to:67890] [flags:0:1:0:0:0] [msg:11:Hello World] [udh:0]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \sa200\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Description\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Date\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 2001-01-01 12:00:00\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Date \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Result\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Sent SMS\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Result: Send, failed, DLR (deliver report), Received, etc. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 SMSC\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 smsc\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Smsc id (\fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0 ) defined in configuration group \fs18\f2 smsc\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 SVC\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 sms\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Service name (\fs18\f2 name\fs20\f0 ) defined in configuration group \fs18\f2 sendsms-user\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 ACT\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 account\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Account name (\fs18\f2 name\fs20\f0 ) defined in configuration group \fs18\f2 sendsms-user\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 BINF\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 binfo\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Billing information used to send this message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Foreign ID\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 1234567890\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Foreign (SMSC-provided) Message ID (only available on MT messages) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 META\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 meta-data\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Meta-data used to send this message. See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_meta_45_data}{\fldrslt Meta Data}} for an explanation about meta-data. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 from\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 12345\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Sender \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 to\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 67890\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Recipient \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Flags\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0:1:0:0:0\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Flags: MClass, Coding, MWI, Compress, DLRMask \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Message Text\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 11:Hello World\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Size of message and message dump (in text or hex if it's binary) \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\cellx3760\clvertalt\cellx6560\clvertalb\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 User Data Header\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 0:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  Size of UDH and UDH Hex dump \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _6638}{\*\bkmkend _6638}\b\fs34\f1 Log rotation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If Kannel is configured so that the bearerbox, wapbox and/or smsbox log to file each of these log files will continue to grow unless administered in some way (this is specially true if access logs are created and/or the log level is set to debug).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A typical way of administering log files is to 'rotate' the logs on a regular basis using a tool such as logrotate. A sample logrotate script (to be added to /etc/logrotate.d) is shown below. In this example the Kannel log files found in /var/log/kannel are rotated and compressed daily over 365 days. See the documentation for logrotate for more details. Of particular note however is the postrotate command, this killall -HUP issues a SIGHUP signal to each Kannel box process running. The SIGHUP signal has the effect of reopening the log file, and performing graceful restart of SMSC connections. Without this command Kannel will continue to write to the rotated log file. Kannel bearerbox can use the SIGUSR2 to reopen the logs only, without performing the graceful restart. This may be more applicable for logrotate usage.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b\fs16\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        /var/log/kannel/*.log \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              daily\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              missingok\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              rotate 365\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              compress\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              delaycompress\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              notifempty\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              create 640 kannel adm\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              sharedscripts\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              postrotate\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                    killall -USR2 bearerbox && killall -HUP smsbox wapbox || true > /dev/null 2> /dev/null\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
              endscript\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Glossary}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Glossary}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _6644}{\*\bkmkend _6644}\b\fs41\f1 Glossary\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 M\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\s3\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart ID_MO}{\*\bkmkend ID_MO}\fs20 MO\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \b0\lang1033\f0 Mobile Originated - a SMS from mobile to application\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb150\s3\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart ID_MT}{\*\bkmkend ID_MT}\b\lang1024\f1 MT\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \b0\lang1033\f0 Mobile Terminated - a SMS from application to mobile\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb150\s3\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart ID_MWI}{\*\bkmkend ID_MWI}\b\lang1024\f1 MWI\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \b0\lang1033\f0 Message Waiting Indicator (See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_Biblio_45_3GPP_45_23038}{\fldrslt [Biblio-3GPP-23038]}})\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb150\s3\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart ID_MCLASS}{\*\bkmkend ID_MCLASS}\b\lang1024\f1 MClass\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \b0\lang1033\f0 Message Class (See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_Biblio_45_3GPP_45_23038}{\fldrslt [Biblio-3GPP-23038]}})\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb150\s3\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart ID_CODING}{\*\bkmkend ID_CODING}\b\lang1024\f1 Coding\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \b0\lang1033\f0 Message Coding (See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_Biblio_45_3GPP_45_23038}{\fldrslt [Biblio-3GPP-23038]}})\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Bibliography}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Bibliography}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _6671}{\*\bkmkend _6671}\b\fs41\f1 Bibliography\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\*\bkmkstart ID_Biblio_45_RFC_45_2616}{\*\bkmkend ID_Biblio_45_RFC_45_2616}\b0\i\fs20\f0 RFC 2616 - Hypertext Transfer Protocol -- HTTP/1.1\i0 , http://www.w3.org/Protocols/rfc2616/rfc2616.html, \i Request for Comments: 2616\i0 , The Internet Society, 1999.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\*\bkmkstart ID_Biblio_45_3GPP_45_23038}{\*\bkmkend ID_Biblio_45_3GPP_45_23038}\i 3GPP 23.038\i0 , http://www.3gpp.org/ftp/Specs/latest/Rel-5/23_series/23038-500.zip, \i ...\i0 , 3GPP, ?.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\*\bkmkstart ID_Biblio_45_3GPP_45_23040}{\*\bkmkend ID_Biblio_45_3GPP_45_23040}\i 3GPP 23.040\i0 , http://www.3gpp.org/ftp/Specs/latest/Rel-5/23_series/23040-530.zip, \i ...\i0 , 3GPP, ?.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1440\sl260\fi-480 {\*\bkmkstart ID_Biblio_45_regexp}{\*\bkmkend ID_Biblio_45_regexp}\i regex(7), GNU regex manual: \i0 , GNU, 1998.\hyphpar0\par}
